78dfed92-16b7-45f3-88c2-3033afb66957	2a8ebf9c-20a2-4c39-b191-840b012371da	voice	{"restricted_phrases": "Audit and limit high-impact rhetorical phrases to a single canonical placement across the site. 'Actually works' should appear only in the index hero or features section. 'Real world' should appear only in the services page. 'Not just in demos' should appear only in the case studies context. Flag these phrases during content review and replace duplicates with specific, concrete alternatives that demonstrate the same confidence through evidence rather than assertion — e.g. replace a second instance of 'actually works' with a specific metric, deployment detail, or named outcome instead."}	content-gap-planner	content-gap-planner	\N	\N	f	2026-03-18 02:23:14.447079+00	2026-03-18 02:25:23.878755+00	content-gap-planner	f
a0f58f5c-7ac8-4220-b295-0fe69a9016cc	e1e22a7d-0552-405a-85b3-1b1e51384df5	content_direction	{"voice": "Direct, confident, slightly provocative. Write like a game announcer who respects the player's intelligence. Short sentences. Declarative statements. No hedging, no 'we believe', no 'our mission is'. State what it is. State what you do. Make it sound like entering an arena, not reading a brochure. Contractions are fine. Sentence fragments are fine. Energy over grammar. The copy should make you want to play, not read more.", "emphasis": "The daily game loop — come back every day, play your take, earn your archetype. The fact that AI never performs, only produces — humans provide all the wit. Archetypes earned from behaviour, not chosen. The contrast with existing platforms (TikTok = watch, Twitter = shout, Spark = play). The provocation as the atomic unit — one question, one take, one timer. The Gauntlet as the gateway experience — 5 minutes to discover who you are.", "cta_style": {"approach": "CTAs are game entry points, not conversion asks. They should feel like the velvet rope dropping, not a form submission. Primary CTAs put the player in motion immediately. Secondary CTAs lead to the Gauntlet as the next best action. Never use passive or corporate CTA language.", "verb_choices": "Play, Enter, Take, Drop, Compete, Claim, Show, Step in, Go — active, physical, game-native verbs. Avoid: Sign up, Learn more, Get started, Discover, Explore, Join us."}, "terminology": {"approach": "Game-native vocabulary used consistently and without apology. Terms are introduced through context, not definition — the experience teaches the vocabulary. Avoid social media terminology entirely. Platform-specific terms should feel like they belong to Spark's world, not borrowed from competitors.", "key_terms": ["Provocation — the daily challenge or question that drives play", "Take — a player's submitted opinion or response", "Gauntlet — the daily 5-provocation timed quiz format", "Archetype — the behaviour-emergent identity earned through play", "Arena — competitive mode where takes are judged head-to-head", "Stage — showcase mode for creative and skill-based play", "Room — a live challenge space, not a feed or page", "Game master — the AI's role: framing, scoring, curating", "Round — a single provocation cycle", "Lobby — the pre-play entry state, used for homepage energy"]}, "avoid_phrases": ["Welcome to", "We believe", "Our mission", "Passionate about", "Leveraging AI", "Powered by cutting-edge", "Join our community", "Sign up today", "Newsletter", "Stay updated", "Learn more about our", "Best-in-class", "Revolutionary", "Disrupting", "Synergy", "Ecosystem", "Content creator", "Influencer", "User-generated content", "Social media platform", "AI-powered content generation"], "blog_strategy": "The provocations feed IS the blog strategy. Each daily provocation is an indexable, shareable content unit tied to a real-world event or trending topic. No editorial blog posts, no thought leadership, no 'how to use Spark' guides. The content is the game material itself. SEO value accumulates through daily provocation generation covering trending cultural topics. Each provocation URL is designed to capture search traffic from people looking for takes on that specific topic. The Daily Gauntlet results page with archetype cards drives social sharing which drives backlinks and referral traffic.", "content_depth": {"thoroughness": "Surface-level by design on most pages — the game is the depth. Explanations should be the minimum needed to get someone into the experience. About page goes slightly deeper but still prioritises energy over exhaustiveness.", "assumed_knowledge": "Readers know TikTok, Twitter, Reddit, and Wordle. They understand social platforms and daily games. They don't need the concept of a 'take' explained. They do need to understand what makes Spark different from those things — which is the game mechanic, not the content.", "explanation_pattern": "Contrast first — establish what Spark is NOT before or alongside what it IS. Use platform comparisons as shorthand. Then introduce the mechanic with the minimum words. Let the interface demonstrate the rest."}, "heading_style": {"format": "Imperative or provocative statements preferred over descriptive labels. H1s should read like opening moves in a game — they challenge or claim something. H2s can be slightly more explanatory but still punchy. Avoid heading-as-label pattern (e.g. 'About Our Platform') — use heading-as-statement (e.g. 'The World Is the Content. Your Take Is the Game.').", "hierarchy": "H1 owns the page energy — one per page, maximum impact. H2s structure the experience into named zones that feel like game sections. H3s used only inside content-heavy pages like About or Archetypes. Never use headings as navigation labels — they should add meaning, not just organise."}, "writing_rules": ["Every headline must create tension or pose a challenge — never describe, always provoke", "Never use the word 'content' — say 'takes', 'provocations', 'plays', or 'moves' instead", "Never describe the AI as creative, funny, or opinionated — it frames, scores, and curates only", "The word 'post' is forbidden — users play, respond, submit, enter, or compete", "Avoid 'community' and 'engage' — use 'arena', 'room', 'play', 'compete', 'show up'", "Archetypes are earned, never chosen — copy must never imply users pick or select their archetype", "Timer and ephemeral mechanics must feel like a feature, never a pressure tactic — frame urgency as energy, not scarcity", "Platform comparisons (TikTok/Twitter) are a core rhetorical tool — use them precisely and confidently, never apologetically", "The game metaphor must be consistent across all pages — 'lobby', 'arena', 'stage', 'round', 'play' not 'section', 'area', 'page', 'step'", "CTAs must be action-first and game-specific — 'Enter the Arena', 'Take the Gauntlet', 'Drop Your Take' not 'Sign Up' or 'Learn More'", "Avoid adjective stacking — one strong word beats three weak ones. 'Electric' not 'fun and exciting and engaging'", "When introducing a mechanic, state what it does before what it is — lead with the player experience, not the feature name"], "sentence_style": {"rhythm": "Staccato with occasional longer sentences for contrast. Like a beat drop — short short short, then a line that breathes. Copy should feel like it has tempo.", "average_length": "Short. Punchy. The game moves fast and so does the copy.", "structure_patterns": "Lead with the action or the stakes, not the context. Fragments used for emphasis. Two-sentence rhythm common: setup then hit. Questions used sparingly but effectively — only when they genuinely challenge the reader."}, "example_phrases": {"characteristic": ["The world happened today. What's your take?", "Five provocations. Five minutes. Find out what kind of player you are.", "You don't scroll here. You show up.", "Your archetype isn't chosen. It's earned.", "The AI sets the table. You bring the heat."], "would_never_say": ["Join our growing community of engaged users", "Our AI-powered platform helps you share your thoughts", "Sign up today to start creating content", "We're passionate about connecting people through meaningful conversations", "Discover a new way to engage with trending topics"]}, "paragraph_style": {"structure": "Point first, always. No throat-clearing. No 'In today's world...' openers. State the thing, then support it briefly if needed. Paragraphs should be able to stand alone — each one lands a single idea.", "typical_length": "1-3 sentences maximum for interface-adjacent copy. About and Archetypes pages allow up to 4-5 sentences per paragraph. Never more. If a paragraph needs a sixth sentence, it should be two paragraphs."}, "things_to_avoid": ["Social media vocabulary: 'post', 'scroll', 'feed', 'followers', 'likes', 'content creator', 'influencer'", "Startup clichés: 'disrupting', 'revolutionising', 'changing the game' (ironic given the actual game metaphor), 'next-generation', 'innovative solution'", "AI hype language: never imply the AI is witty, creative, or opinionated — it is infrastructure, not a personality", "Passive or vague CTAs: 'learn more', 'find out how', 'get started', 'explore our platform'", "Corporate warmth: 'we're passionate about', 'our community', 'connecting people', 'bringing people together'", "Overexplaining the mechanics before letting the player experience them — trust the interface", "Dark, violent, or gruesome framing even when provocations are edgy — competitive and adult is not the same as hostile", "Generic social proof: 'millions of users', 'loved by creators', 'trusted by thousands' — unverifiable claims undermine the earned-reputation ethos"], "compliance_rules": [], "things_to_emulate": ["Wordle's zero-explanation entry — the interface teaches the game, copy just opens the door", "The Economist's headline confidence — states a position without hedging, trusts the reader to engage or push back", "Duolingo's streak mechanic language — makes daily return feel like momentum, not obligation", "Sports commentary economy — maximum information, minimum words, always in motion", "The way a great game menu feels — every option is a clear action, nothing is ambiguous, energy is immediate", "Hot Takes Twitter at its best — sharp, specific, committed to the bit without being mean-spirited", "Arcade game copy conventions — score, time, round, level — language that creates stakes without explaining them"], "social_proof_style": "No testimonials in v1 — the platform has no users yet and fabricated quotes would destroy credibility. Social proof comes from the game itself: show provocation count, active player count (when available), and archetype distribution stats. In v1, the proof is the experience — get them into the Gauntlet and let the archetype card be the proof. Future versions will use archetype card shares as organic social proof.", "persuasion_approach": {"method": "The game sells itself by being immediately playable. Copy doesn't persuade — it provokes. The CTA is the first provocation card. If the reader has a take, they're already in. Persuasion happens through participation, not description.", "trust_building": "Trust is built through the mechanics being exactly what they say they are. No bait and switch. The timer is real. The archetype is earned. The provocation is genuinely provocative. Credibility comes from the experience being what the copy promised.", "social_proof_style": "Archetype cards shared by real players are the social proof. In v1, lean on the specificity and credibility of the archetypes themselves — if the archetype description rings true, it earns trust. No fake testimonials. No vague 'thousands of players' claims."}}	build-site-planner	build-site-planner	\N	\N	f	2026-04-02 11:46:10.622762+00	2026-04-02 14:08:35.291069+00	build-site-planner	f
e02da92c-f8d7-4cab-a9b7-5195510d1f9b	1368e337-dd1d-4799-bbb3-8221a1b79bcc	briefing	{"tagline": "From idea to AI system", "about_us": "  Finetune is a small technical company focused on practical AI deployment.  We build systems that combine automation, data pipelines, model training, and agent-based architectures.  Our aim is to make AI genuinely useful.   ## AI systems that actually do the work  Most companies experimenting with AI end up with prototypes, demos, or chatbots.  Very few end up with systems that **save time, automate work, or generate measurable value**.  Finetune helps organisations design and deploy practical AI systems — automation pipelines, specialised models, and intelligent agents that solve real operational problems.  Not AI theatre.  ---  Real systems.  # Rapid experimentation  AI technology is moving quickly.  The companies that benefit most are those that can **experiment cheaply and deploy quickly**.  We aim to make it easy to test ideas, build prototypes, and evolve them into real systems when they prove useful.   ---  # What we build  We work with companies to build AI infrastructure that fits into real workflows.  ### Automation systems  AI that screens requests, processes documents, extracts information, and routes work automatically.  ### Intelligent agent pipelines  Systems where multiple AI agents collaborate to solve problems, analyse information, or complete complex tasks.  ### Custom AI models  Fine-tuned models trained on domain-specific data using techniques such as LoRA and other efficient training methods.  ### RAG systems for company knowledge  AI that can search and reason over internal data such as documents, emails, financial information, and knowledge bases.  ### Private AI deployments  Secure systems where models can run locally or within controlled environments to protect sensitive data.  ### Data collection infrastructure  Large-scale web data pipelines capable of gathering structured information from thousands of sources.  ---  # Why most AI projects fail  Many AI initiatives stall for predictable reasons.  **They start with tools instead of problems.**  **They stay in prototype mode.**  **They rely on third-party systems that can’t integrate with existing workflows.**  **Or they assume a single AI model will magically solve everything.**  In practice, useful AI systems usually require:  * data pipelines * automation layers * model adaptation * monitoring and iteration  AI works best as **part of a system**, not as a standalone tool.  ---  # The Finetune approach  We focus on **practical deployment**.  That means starting with a real operational problem and designing a system around it.  A typical project might involve:  1. Collecting and structuring the relevant data 2. Building automation pipelines 3. Training or adapting models 4. Integrating the system into existing workflows 5. Iterating until the system reliably saves time or improves decisions  The goal is not simply to use AI.  The goal is to make something **genuinely useful**.  ---  # What we can help with  Examples of problems we help solve:  * Screening and routing incoming requests * Automating quoting or proposal workflows * Extracting structured information from documents * Building internal knowledge assistants * Collecting market or financial data at scale * Deploying private AI models for sensitive workflows * Setting up agent pipelines that can perform complex multi-step tasks  Some systems are simple and highly targeted.  Others involve networks of cooperating agents and large-scale data pipelines.  ---  # A note on scale  We are particularly interested in **agent systems and large-scale automation**.  This includes experimentation with environments where thousands of AI agents can communicate and collaborate to solve problems.  These systems are still emerging, but they point toward a future where much of the routine work inside organisations can be handled by coordinated software agents.  Our goal is to help companies begin exploring that future in a practical way.  ---    ---  # Who we work with  We tend to work best with organisations that:  * have complex workflows or data * are open to experimentation * want to build internal capability with AI rather than just outsource it  This includes startups, established companies, and technical teams exploring new ways to operate.  ---  # About Finetune  Finetune is a small technical company focused on practical AI deployment.  We build systems that combine automation, data pipelines, model training, and agent-based architectures.  Our focus is simple:  **make AI genuinely useful inside real organisations.**  ---  # Call to action  ## Curious what AI could automate in your business?  We’re always happy to have a technical conversation about possible approaches.  Contact us to discuss your workflows, data, or ideas.", "services": [{"name": "AI Automation Systems  ", "description": "Design and deployment of AI-driven automation pipelines that remove manual work from business processes. Examples include request screening, automated quoting, document analysis, and workflow orchestration."}, {"name": "Intelligent Agent Systems", "description": "Design and deployment of networks of AI agents capable of solving complex tasks collaboratively. These systems can analyse information, communicate with each other, and perform large-scale problem solving. Talk to us about systems capable of running tens of thousands of AI agents communicating with each other to solve complex tasks."}, {"name": "Custom AI Model Training", "description": "Training specialised models using techniques such as LoRA, DoRA, and other parameter-efficient methods to adapt modern AI models to your company's specific needs."}, {"name": "RAG Systems for Company Data", "description": "Deployment of Retrieval-Augmented Generation systems that allow AI to search and reason over your internal data including:  Emails  Documents  Financial records  Marketing material  Knowledge bases"}, {"name": "Bulk Data Collection", "description": "Large-scale web data extraction across thousands of websites, including structured business and financial data collection."}, {"name": "Financial and Company Data Pipelines", "description": "Automated systems for collecting and analysing financial data from public sources including company registries and regulatory filings."}, {"name": "Private AI Deployments", "description": "Secure deployment of AI systems that run privately — including models that can run locally or in-browser to protect company data and reduce usage costs."}, {"name": "Agent Pipeline Infrastructure", "description": "Installation and configuration of agent frameworks and orchestration systems so companies can run their own AI agents in production."}, {"name": "Rapid Website Generation", "description": "Creation of complex, high-quality static websites within minutes for:  company identity  marketing pages  landing pages  documentation  presentations"}, {"name": "AI Strategy & Implementation", "description": "Hands-on support helping companies move from curiosity about AI to systems that actually work inside their business."}, {"name": "High-Volume Data Acquisition", "description": "Design systems capable of collecting structured information from thousands of websites automatically."}], "company_name": "FineTuning"}	pageflow-builder	briefing-agent	\N	\N	f	2026-03-20 01:20:06.744605+00	2026-03-20 02:01:46.171438+00	briefing-agent	f
2d660203-22df-4b19-8650-f52a0d7737fb	1368e337-dd1d-4799-bbb3-8221a1b79bcc	briefing	{"tagline": "From idea to AI system", "about_us": "  Finetune is a small technical company focused on practical AI deployment.  We build systems that combine automation, data pipelines, model training, and agent-based architectures.  Our aim is to make AI genuinely useful.   ## AI systems that actually do the work  Most companies experimenting with AI end up with prototypes, demos, or chatbots.  Very few end up with systems that **save time, automate work, or generate measurable value**.  Finetune helps organisations design and deploy practical AI systems — automation pipelines, specialised models, and intelligent agents that solve real operational problems.  Not AI theatre.  ---  Real systems.  # Rapid experimentation  AI technology is moving quickly.  The companies that benefit most are those that can **experiment cheaply and deploy quickly**.  We aim to make it easy to test ideas, build prototypes, and evolve them into real systems when they prove useful.   ---  # What we build  We work with companies to build AI infrastructure that fits into real workflows.  ### Automation systems  AI that screens requests, processes documents, extracts information, and routes work automatically.  ### Intelligent agent pipelines  Systems where multiple AI agents collaborate to solve problems, analyse information, or complete complex tasks.  ### Custom AI models  Fine-tuned models trained on domain-specific data using techniques such as LoRA and other efficient training methods.  ### RAG systems for company knowledge  AI that can search and reason over internal data such as documents, emails, financial information, and knowledge bases.  ### Private AI deployments  Secure systems where models can run locally or within controlled environments to protect sensitive data.  ### Data collection infrastructure  Large-scale web data pipelines capable of gathering structured information from thousands of sources.  ---  # Why most AI projects fail  Many AI initiatives stall for predictable reasons.  **They start with tools instead of problems.**  **They stay in prototype mode.**  **They rely on third-party systems that can’t integrate with existing workflows.**  **Or they assume a single AI model will magically solve everything.**  In practice, useful AI systems usually require:  * data pipelines * automation layers * model adaptation * monitoring and iteration  AI works best as **part of a system**, not as a standalone tool.  ---  # The Finetune approach  We focus on **practical deployment**.  That means starting with a real operational problem and designing a system around it.  A typical project might involve:  1. Collecting and structuring the relevant data 2. Building automation pipelines 3. Training or adapting models 4. Integrating the system into existing workflows 5. Iterating until the system reliably saves time or improves decisions  The goal is not simply to use AI.  The goal is to make something **genuinely useful**.  ---  # What we can help with  Examples of problems we help solve:  * Screening and routing incoming requests * Automating quoting or proposal workflows * Extracting structured information from documents * Building internal knowledge assistants * Collecting market or financial data at scale * Deploying private AI models for sensitive workflows * Setting up agent pipelines that can perform complex multi-step tasks  Some systems are simple and highly targeted.  Others involve networks of cooperating agents and large-scale data pipelines.  ---  # A note on scale  We are particularly interested in **agent systems and large-scale automation**.  This includes experimentation with environments where thousands of AI agents can communicate and collaborate to solve problems.  These systems are still emerging, but they point toward a future where much of the routine work inside organisations can be handled by coordinated software agents.  Our goal is to help companies begin exploring that future in a practical way.  ---    ---  # Who we work with  We tend to work best with organisations that:  * have complex workflows or data * are open to experimentation * want to build internal capability with AI rather than just outsource it  This includes startups, established companies, and technical teams exploring new ways to operate.  ---  # About Finetune  Finetune is a small technical company focused on practical AI deployment.  We build systems that combine automation, data pipelines, model training, and agent-based architectures.  Our focus is simple:  **make AI genuinely useful inside real organisations.**  ---  # Call to action  ## Curious what AI could automate in your business?  We’re always happy to have a technical conversation about possible approaches.  Contact us to discuss your workflows, data, or ideas.", "services": [{"name": "AI Automation Systems  ", "description": "Design and deployment of AI-driven automation pipelines that remove manual work from business processes. Examples include request screening, automated quoting, document analysis, and workflow orchestration."}, {"name": "Intelligent Agent Systems", "description": "Design and deployment of networks of AI agents capable of solving complex tasks collaboratively. These systems can analyse information, communicate with each other, and perform large-scale problem solving. Talk to us about systems capable of running tens of thousands of AI agents communicating with each other to solve complex tasks."}, {"name": "Custom AI Model Training", "description": "Training specialised models using techniques such as LoRA, DoRA, and other parameter-efficient methods to adapt modern AI models to your company's specific needs."}, {"name": "RAG Systems for Company Data", "description": "Deployment of Retrieval-Augmented Generation systems that allow AI to search and reason over your internal data including:  Emails  Documents  Financial records  Marketing material  Knowledge bases"}, {"name": "Bulk Data Collection", "description": "Large-scale web data extraction across thousands of websites, including structured business and financial data collection."}, {"name": "Financial and Company Data Pipelines", "description": "Automated systems for collecting and analysing financial data from public sources including company registries and regulatory filings."}, {"name": "Private AI Deployments", "description": "Secure deployment of AI systems that run privately — including models that can run locally or in-browser to protect company data and reduce usage costs."}, {"name": "Agent Pipeline Infrastructure", "description": "Installation and configuration of agent frameworks and orchestration systems so companies can run their own AI agents in production."}, {"name": "Rapid Website Generation", "description": "Creation of complex, high-quality static websites within minutes for:  company identity  marketing pages  landing pages  documentation  presentations"}, {"name": "AI Strategy & Implementation", "description": "Hands-on support helping companies move from curiosity about AI to systems that actually work inside their business."}, {"name": "High-Volume Data Acquisition", "description": "Design systems capable of collecting structured information from thousands of websites automatically."}], "company_name": "FineTuning"}	pageflow-builder	briefing-agent	\N	\N	f	2026-03-20 02:01:46.171438+00	2026-03-20 12:39:53.363937+00	briefing-agent	f
