18957db7-ff15-4821-9356-290613bfe405	4851f6fc-71cf-4160-a270-e03d6d3e0732	voice_and_messaging	{"time_promise_claims": "All delivery timeline claims must use a single canonical form: 'days, not months'. This reflects actual scoped delivery timelines for AI agent builds and is specific enough to be credible to a technical audience. Variants such as 'minutes, not weeks', 'hours, not days', or 'weeks, not years' are prohibited site-wide as they read as templated filler and create contradictions that undermine trust with CTO-level evaluators. The canonical phrase should appear consistently on index, about, services, and any use-case or case-study pages that reference delivery speed. If a specific engagement genuinely has a different timeline (e.g. a proof-of-concept delivered in under a week), that claim must be scoped explicitly to that context rather than used as a global positioning statement."}	content-gap-planner	content-gap-planner	\N	\N	t	2026-03-15 00:28:08.271736+00	\N	content-gap-planner	f
c8f49c7c-f406-4860-a7db-2f0a3b80b4f6	1368e337-dd1d-4799-bbb3-8221a1b79bcc	identity	{"tone": "conversational, direct, anti-corporate", "email": "finetuning@contactforsales.com", "phone": "+44 (0) 7934 524 911", "tagline": "AI for the Rest of Us", "industry": "AI consulting / technology services", "multiple": "industry: AI consulting and technology services | target_audience: UK SMEs including managing directors, operations managers, and technical founders seeking practical AI implementation | tone: plain-speaking, anti-hype, practitioner-led — direct and honest without jargon or inflated claims | purpose: lead generation for custom AI projects, positioning FineTuning as the credible no-nonsense alternative to big consultancies for businesses that want results not presentations", "company_name": "FineTuning", "content_brief": "Primary audience: UK SME owners and senior managers, typically 5–50 employees, non-technical, sceptical of AI hype, looking for honest and practical guidance on where AI can genuinely save time or money. Tone: plain-spoken, pragmatic, reassuring — the voice of an experienced practitioner who has no interest in overselling. No jargon, no buzzwords, no breathless enthusiasm. Site purpose: generate discovery call bookings by building trust with an audience that has been burned by vague AI promises before. Every page should either educate, reassure, or move the reader toward booking a call. Industry verticals: broad UK SME, with emphasis on professional services, retail, and light manufacturing where operational efficiency gains are most tangible.", "tone_of_voice": "Plain-spoken and direct. We write like experienced practitioners talking to a busy business owner, not like a tech vendor pitching to a CTO. No buzzwords, no hype, no jargon without explanation. Confident but not arrogant. We acknowledge AI limitations honestly. Short sentences preferred. Active voice. UK English spelling. Avoid: 'leverage', 'cutting-edge', 'transformative', 'unlock', 'harness', 'game-changing', 'AI-powered' as a standalone claim. When in doubt, ask: would a sceptical SME owner roll their eyes at this? If yes, rewrite it.", "target_audience": "UK SME owners and senior managers with 5–50 employees, typically non-technical, who are curious about AI but sceptical of hype. They want honest, practical advice on where AI can genuinely save time or money in their business — not a technology lecture. Primary conversion goal is to book a discovery call. Industry verticals are broad but may skew toward professional services, retail, and light manufacturing where operational efficiency gains are most tangible.", "content_strategy": "Target audience: UK SME owners and managers, typically 5–50 employees, non-technical but commercially minded. Tone: plain-spoken, pragmatic, reassuring — no jargon, no hype, honest about what AI can and cannot do. Purpose: lead generation via consultation bookings, supported by trust-building content. Industry: AI consulting and advisory services. All content should speak directly to time-poor business owners who are sceptical of tech vendors and want straight answers.", "editorial_metadata": "target_audience: UK SME owners and senior managers (5–50 employees), non-technical, sceptical of hype, seeking practical AI guidance; tone: plain-speaking, pragmatic, no jargon, experienced-practitioner voice; purpose: lead generation via discovery call bookings; industry: AI consulting and technology services for UK SMEs, with emphasis on professional services, retail, and light manufacturing"}	content-gap-planner	content-gap-planner	\N	\N	f	2026-03-14 22:37:40.362691+00	2026-03-15 01:29:44.61552+00	content-gap-planner	f
acaacb0b-33ff-4e8b-be08-5739537dc154	1368e337-dd1d-4799-bbb3-8221a1b79bcc	briefing	{"tagline": "From idea to AI system", "about_us": "  Finetune is a small technical company focused on practical AI deployment.  We build systems that combine automation, data pipelines, model training, and agent-based architectures.  Our aim is to make AI genuinely useful.   ## AI systems that actually do the work  Most companies experimenting with AI end up with prototypes, demos, or chatbots.  Very few end up with systems that **save time, automate work, or generate measurable value**.  Finetune helps organisations design and deploy practical AI systems — automation pipelines, specialised models, and intelligent agents that solve real operational problems.  Not AI theatre.  ---  Real systems.  # Rapid experimentation  AI technology is moving quickly.  The companies that benefit most are those that can **experiment cheaply and deploy quickly**.  We aim to make it easy to test ideas, build prototypes, and evolve them into real systems when they prove useful.   ---  # What we build  We work with companies to build AI infrastructure that fits into real workflows.  ### Automation systems  AI that screens requests, processes documents, extracts information, and routes work automatically.  ### Intelligent agent pipelines  Systems where multiple AI agents collaborate to solve problems, analyse information, or complete complex tasks.  ### Custom AI models  Fine-tuned models trained on domain-specific data using techniques such as LoRA and other efficient training methods.  ### RAG systems for company knowledge  AI that can search and reason over internal data such as documents, emails, financial information, and knowledge bases.  ### Private AI deployments  Secure systems where models can run locally or within controlled environments to protect sensitive data.  ### Data collection infrastructure  Large-scale web data pipelines capable of gathering structured information from thousands of sources.  ---  # Why most AI projects fail  Many AI initiatives stall for predictable reasons.  **They start with tools instead of problems.**  **They stay in prototype mode.**  **They rely on third-party systems that can’t integrate with existing workflows.**  **Or they assume a single AI model will magically solve everything.**  In practice, useful AI systems usually require:  * data pipelines * automation layers * model adaptation * monitoring and iteration  AI works best as **part of a system**, not as a standalone tool.  ---  # The Finetune approach  We focus on **practical deployment**.  That means starting with a real operational problem and designing a system around it.  A typical project might involve:  1. Collecting and structuring the relevant data 2. Building automation pipelines 3. Training or adapting models 4. Integrating the system into existing workflows 5. Iterating until the system reliably saves time or improves decisions  The goal is not simply to use AI.  The goal is to make something **genuinely useful**.  ---  # What we can help with  Examples of problems we help solve:  * Screening and routing incoming requests * Automating quoting or proposal workflows * Extracting structured information from documents * Building internal knowledge assistants * Collecting market or financial data at scale * Deploying private AI models for sensitive workflows * Setting up agent pipelines that can perform complex multi-step tasks  Some systems are simple and highly targeted.  Others involve networks of cooperating agents and large-scale data pipelines.  ---  # A note on scale  We are particularly interested in **agent systems and large-scale automation**.  This includes experimentation with environments where thousands of AI agents can communicate and collaborate to solve problems.  These systems are still emerging, but they point toward a future where much of the routine work inside organisations can be handled by coordinated software agents.  Our goal is to help companies begin exploring that future in a practical way.  ---    ---  # Who we work with  We tend to work best with organisations that:  * have complex workflows or data * are open to experimentation * want to build internal capability with AI rather than just outsource it  This includes startups, established companies, and technical teams exploring new ways to operate.  ---  # About Finetune  Finetune is a small technical company focused on practical AI deployment.  We build systems that combine automation, data pipelines, model training, and agent-based architectures.  Our focus is simple:  **make AI genuinely useful inside real organisations.**  ---  # Call to action  ## Curious what AI could automate in your business?  We’re always happy to have a technical conversation about possible approaches.  Contact us to discuss your workflows, data, or ideas.", "services": [{"name": "AI Automation Systems  ", "description": "Design and deployment of AI-driven automation pipelines that remove manual work from business processes. Examples include request screening, automated quoting, document analysis, and workflow orchestration."}, {"name": "Intelligent Agent Systems", "description": "Design and deployment of networks of AI agents capable of solving complex tasks collaboratively. These systems can analyse information, communicate with each other, and perform large-scale problem solving. Talk to us about systems capable of running tens of thousands of AI agents communicating with each other to solve complex tasks."}, {"name": "Custom AI Model Training", "description": "Training specialised models using techniques such as LoRA, DoRA, and other parameter-efficient methods to adapt modern AI models to your company's specific needs."}, {"name": "RAG Systems for Company Data", "description": "Deployment of Retrieval-Augmented Generation systems that allow AI to search and reason over your internal data including:  Emails  Documents  Financial records  Marketing material  Knowledge bases"}, {"name": "Bulk Data Collection", "description": "Large-scale web data extraction across thousands of websites, including structured business and financial data collection."}, {"name": "Financial and Company Data Pipelines", "description": "Automated systems for collecting and analysing financial data from public sources including company registries and regulatory filings."}, {"name": "Private AI Deployments", "description": "Secure deployment of AI systems that run privately — including models that can run locally or in-browser to protect company data and reduce usage costs."}, {"name": "Agent Pipeline Infrastructure", "description": "Installation and configuration of agent frameworks and orchestration systems so companies can run their own AI agents in production."}, {"name": "Rapid Website Generation", "description": "Creation of complex, high-quality static websites within minutes for:  company identity  marketing pages  landing pages  documentation  presentations"}, {"name": "AI Strategy & Implementation", "description": "Hands-on support helping companies move from curiosity about AI to systems that actually work inside their business."}, {"name": "High-Volume Data Acquisition", "description": "Design systems capable of collecting structured information from thousands of websites automatically."}], "company_name": "FineTuning"}	pageflow-builder	briefing-agent	\N	\N	f	2026-03-21 11:15:23.039018+00	2026-03-21 11:56:53.963921+00	briefing-agent	f
ac6987bf-7684-4aa5-8954-6382cf893d0a	1368e337-dd1d-4799-bbb3-8221a1b79bcc	identity	{"tone": "conversational, direct, anti-corporate", "email": "finetuning@contactforsales.com", "phone": "+44 (0) 7934 524 911", "tagline": "AI for the Rest of Us", "industry": "AI consulting / technology services", "multiple": "industry: AI consulting and technology services | target_audience: UK SMEs including managing directors, operations managers, and technical founders seeking practical AI implementation | tone: plain-speaking, anti-hype, practitioner-led — direct and honest without jargon or inflated claims | purpose: lead generation for custom AI projects, positioning FineTuning as the credible no-nonsense alternative to big consultancies for businesses that want results not presentations", "company_name": "FineTuning", "content_brief": "Primary audience: UK SME owners and senior managers, typically 5–50 employees, non-technical, sceptical of AI hype, looking for honest and practical guidance on where AI can genuinely save time or money. Tone: plain-spoken, pragmatic, reassuring — the voice of an experienced practitioner who has no interest in overselling. No jargon, no buzzwords, no breathless enthusiasm. Site purpose: generate discovery call bookings by building trust with an audience that has been burned by vague AI promises before. Every page should either educate, reassure, or move the reader toward booking a call. Industry verticals: broad UK SME, with emphasis on professional services, retail, and light manufacturing where operational efficiency gains are most tangible.", "tone_of_voice": "Plain-spoken and direct. We write like experienced practitioners talking to a busy business owner, not like a tech vendor pitching to a CTO. No buzzwords, no hype, no jargon without explanation. Confident but not arrogant. We acknowledge AI limitations honestly. Short sentences preferred. Active voice. UK English spelling. Avoid: 'leverage', 'cutting-edge', 'transformative', 'unlock', 'harness', 'game-changing', 'AI-powered' as a standalone claim. When in doubt, ask: would a sceptical SME owner roll their eyes at this? If yes, rewrite it.", "target_audience": "UK SME owners and senior managers at businesses with 5–50 employees. Typically non-technical. Curious about AI but sceptical of vendor hype. Want honest, practical guidance on where AI can save time or money — not a technology lecture. Primary conversion action is booking a discovery call.", "content_strategy": "Target audience: UK SME owners and managers, typically 5–50 employees, non-technical but commercially minded. Tone: plain-spoken, pragmatic, reassuring — no jargon, no hype, honest about what AI can and cannot do. Purpose: lead generation via consultation bookings, supported by trust-building content. Industry: AI consulting and advisory services. All content should speak directly to time-poor business owners who are sceptical of tech vendors and want straight answers.", "editorial_metadata": "target_audience: UK SME owners and senior managers (5–50 employees), non-technical, sceptical of hype, seeking practical AI guidance; tone: plain-speaking, pragmatic, no jargon, experienced-practitioner voice; purpose: lead generation via discovery call bookings; industry: AI consulting and technology services for UK SMEs, with emphasis on professional services, retail, and light manufacturing"}	content-gap-planner	content-gap-planner	\N	\N	f	2026-03-15 01:29:44.622444+00	2026-03-15 01:30:36.036913+00	content-gap-planner	f
28d1a415-d18a-4f35-9de6-e4b6070200af	15a6cb16-5a86-4541-a8e4-d7106239b6a4	content_direction	{"voice": {"person": "Second person ('you') is used consistently for direct address, positioning the reader as an active practitioner; third person is used when describing tools or concepts as objects; first person is entirely absent, removing the author from the equation and centering the reader and the work.", "register": "Professional-technical with a sharp, commanding edge — the site speaks like a senior game designer who has no patience for vagueness and treats precision as a core value, not a stylistic choice.", "formality": "Mid-register — formal enough to use precise technical vocabulary and avoid colloquialisms, but not academic or stiff. Contractions are absent. Sentences are declarative and clipped. The tone resembles a confident technical brief rather than either a casual blog or a formal whitepaper.", "emotional_tone": "Challenging and empowering simultaneously — the reader is made to feel that they have been doing things the wrong way (guessing, using spreadsheets, not understanding the 'why') and that this site is the corrective. There is no warmth or hand-holding, but there is respect for the reader's intelligence and ambition.", "authority_level": "Authority is established through specificity — naming exact methodologies (Monte Carlo simulations, Lanchester combat models, EHP calculations), using correct technical vocabulary without apology, and framing tools as professional-grade instruments rather than hobbyist aids. The site never claims to be the best; it simply demonstrates competence through the precision of its language."}, "formatted": "Heading style:\\nExamples from site:\\n- Design with Authority.\\n- Open on Your Second Monitor. Built for the Work.\\n- The 'Why' Behind the 'What'\\n- Everything You Need to Design Better Games\\n- Stop Guessing. Start Calculating.\\nFormat: Headings are short, declarative, and commanding. H1s are bold brand statements or imperative directives — often a single short sentence or a two-part construction separated by a period. H2s frame sections as benefits or capabilities. H3s are noun-phrase labels naming specific tools or concepts. Headings never end in question marks and rarely exceed 10 words.\\nHierarchy: H1 is a single commanding brand statement per page. H2 introduces major content sections with a benefit or capability framing. H3 labels individual tools, features, or topics as named entities — functioning more like product names than descriptive headings.\\n\\nParagraph style:\\nTypical length: 2–4 sentences. Rarely longer. Single-sentence paragraphs appear in hero sections for maximum impact.\\nStructure: Lead with the core claim or function, then add one or two sentences of elaboration or specification. No throat-clearing or contextual preamble — the first sentence is always the most important.\\nOpening patterns: Paragraphs frequently open with an imperative ('Stop using spreadsheets'), a declarative reframe ('Health is a lie'), a capability statement ('Access a suite of...'), or a direct noun phrase naming the subject ('A macro-economic sandbox for...').\\n\\nVoice:\\nEmotional tone: Challenging and empowering simultaneously — the reader is made to feel that they have been doing things the wrong way (guessing, using spreadsheets, not understanding the 'why') and that this site is the corrective. There is no warmth or hand-holding, but there is respect for the reader's intelligence and ambition.\\nFormality: Mid-register — formal enough to use precise technical vocabulary and avoid colloquialisms, but not academic or stiff. Contractions are absent. Sentences are declarative and clipped. The tone resembles a confident technical brief rather than either a casual blog or a formal whitepaper.\\nRegister: Professional-technical with a sharp, commanding edge — the site speaks like a senior game designer who has no patience for vagueness and treats precision as a core value, not a stylistic choice.\\nPerson: Second person ('you') is used consistently for direct address, positioning the reader as an active practitioner; third person is used when describing tools or concepts as objects; first person is entirely absent, removing the author from the equation and centering the reader and the work.\\nAuthority level: Authority is established through specificity — naming exact methodologies (Monte Carlo simulations, Lanchester combat models, EHP calculations), using correct technical vocabulary without apology, and framing tools as professional-grade instruments rather than hobbyist aids. The site never claims to be the best; it simply demonstrates competence through the precision of its language.\\n\\nSentence style:\\nAverage length: Short to medium — typically 10–20 words per sentence. Sentences are rarely compound-complex. Many key statements are deliberately short (under 10 words) for impact.\\nStructure patterns: Declarative statements dominate. Imperative constructions are used for commands and CTAs. The site frequently uses the pattern: [bold claim] + [supporting elaboration in the next sentence]. Fragments are used intentionally for rhetorical punch ('No installs, no data stored — just the numbers'). Em dashes are used to pivot from setup to payoff within a single sentence.\\nRhythm: Staccato and purposeful — short declarative sentences are followed by slightly longer elaborative sentences, creating a push-pull rhythm. The cadence mimics a confident verbal briefing: assertion, then context, then implication. Lists of three items appear frequently, reinforcing a structured, methodical voice.\\nConnectives: Minimal connective tissue between sentences — ideas are juxtaposed rather than linked with transitional phrases. 'And' is occasionally used mid-sentence. 'From... to...' constructions are used to establish range and scope. Paragraphs do not open with 'However', 'Therefore', or other formal academic connectives.\\n\\nPersuasion approach:\\nMethod: The site persuades by diagnosing a problem the reader may not have named ('Stop guessing', 'Health is a lie', 'Stop using spreadsheets') and then positioning its tools as the logical, precise solution. It does not use testimonials, case studies, or emotional appeals — it persuades through demonstrated technical credibility and the implicit promise of professional-grade output.\\nTrust building: Trust is built through technical specificity (naming exact algorithms, simulation types, and output formats like Unity/Unreal compatibility), through transparency about what the tools do and don't do ('no installs, no data stored'), and through the consistent framing of the site as a utility rather than a product — something built for the work, not for the sale.\\nSocial proof style: Social proof is entirely absent from the provided content. No testimonials, user counts, client names, or community references appear. The site relies entirely on the implied authority of its technical depth.\\n\\nContent depth:\\nAssumed knowledge: The reader is assumed to be a practising game designer or developer with working knowledge of game systems concepts. Terms like 'TTK', 'EHP', 'Monte Carlo', 'faucets and sinks', 'pity systems', and 'Lanchester models' are used without definition or apology. The reader is not a beginner.\\nExplanation pattern: Complex ideas are introduced by naming them precisely, then immediately stating their practical design relevance. No analogies or metaphors are used to simplify. Instead, the site uses reframing ('Health is a lie. Calculate true survivability...') to make technical concepts feel intuitive through a provocative lens rather than a simplified one.\\nThoroughness: Each tool or guide is introduced at a high level with enough specificity to signal depth without fully delivering it on the index/landing pages. The pattern is: name the concept, name the method, state the practical output. Full depth is reserved for the tool or guide itself, not the marketing copy around it.\\n\\nWriting rules:\\n- Open section headings with imperative commands or provocative declarative statements — never questions. ('Design with Authority.' not 'How Do You Design Better Games?')\\n- Use the second person ('you') for all direct address. Never use first person singular or plural ('I', 'we', 'our team').\\n- Keep paragraphs to 2–4 sentences maximum. Each paragraph carries one idea. Do not blend multiple concepts into a single paragraph.\\n- Use short, punchy sentences (under 10 words) as section openers or closers for rhetorical impact. Follow with a longer elaborative sentence.\\n- Name specific technical methodologies, algorithms, and outputs by their correct names without defining them — the reader is assumed to be a professional who recognises them.\\n- Use em dashes to pivot from a problem statement to its resolution within a single sentence or to add a sharp qualification without a subordinate clause.\\n- Avoid all superlatives ('best', 'most powerful', 'ultimate', 'leading'). Let specificity carry the weight of quality claims.\\n- Frame tools and guides in terms of what the designer can do with them, not what the site provides. Output and application are the focus, not features.\\n- CTAs use action verbs that are informational or exploratory ('Read', 'Browse', 'Explore', 'Access') — never transactional or high-pressure ('Buy', 'Sign Up', 'Get Instant Access').\\n- Repeat key brand phrases across pages to build a consistent vocabulary ('utility engine', 'design with authority', 'serious game designers', 'the why not just the what').\\n- When listing tools or features, use a consistent three-part structure: [name] + [what it does] + [why it matters for design]. Do not vary this pattern within a list.\\n- Use 'From X to Y' constructions to establish the breadth of coverage without enumerating every item.\\n- Avoid hedging language ('might', 'could', 'perhaps', 'we think'). All statements are made with declarative confidence.\\n- Technical output formats (Unity, Unreal, XP tables) should be named explicitly to signal professional-grade applicability.\\n- Never use exclamation marks. Enthusiasm is conveyed through precision and directness, not punctuation.", "content_depth": {"thoroughness": "Each tool or guide is introduced at a high level with enough specificity to signal depth without fully delivering it on the index/landing pages. The pattern is: name the concept, name the method, state the practical output. Full depth is reserved for the tool or guide itself, not the marketing copy around it.", "assumed_knowledge": "The reader is assumed to be a practising game designer or developer with working knowledge of game systems concepts. Terms like 'TTK', 'EHP', 'Monte Carlo', 'faucets and sinks', 'pity systems', and 'Lanchester models' are used without definition or apology. The reader is not a beginner.", "explanation_pattern": "Complex ideas are introduced by naming them precisely, then immediately stating their practical design relevance. No analogies or metaphors are used to simplify. Instead, the site uses reframing ('Health is a lie. Calculate true survivability...') to make technical concepts feel intuitive through a provocative lens rather than a simplified one."}, "heading_style": {"format": "Headings are short, declarative, and commanding. H1s are bold brand statements or imperative directives — often a single short sentence or a two-part construction separated by a period. H2s frame sections as benefits or capabilities. H3s are noun-phrase labels naming specific tools or concepts. Headings never end in question marks and rarely exceed 10 words.", "hierarchy": "H1 is a single commanding brand statement per page. H2 introduces major content sections with a benefit or capability framing. H3 labels individual tools, features, or topics as named entities — functioning more like product names than descriptive headings.", "examples_from_site": ["Design with Authority.", "Open on Your Second Monitor. Built for the Work.", "The 'Why' Behind the 'What'", "Everything You Need to Design Better Games", "Stop Guessing. Start Calculating."]}, "writing_rules": ["Open section headings with imperative commands or provocative declarative statements — never questions. ('Design with Authority.' not 'How Do You Design Better Games?')", "Use the second person ('you') for all direct address. Never use first person singular or plural ('I', 'we', 'our team').", "Keep paragraphs to 2–4 sentences maximum. Each paragraph carries one idea. Do not blend multiple concepts into a single paragraph.", "Use short, punchy sentences (under 10 words) as section openers or closers for rhetorical impact. Follow with a longer elaborative sentence.", "Name specific technical methodologies, algorithms, and outputs by their correct names without defining them — the reader is assumed to be a professional who recognises them.", "Use em dashes to pivot from a problem statement to its resolution within a single sentence or to add a sharp qualification without a subordinate clause.", "Avoid all superlatives ('best', 'most powerful', 'ultimate', 'leading'). Let specificity carry the weight of quality claims.", "Frame tools and guides in terms of what the designer can do with them, not what the site provides. Output and application are the focus, not features.", "CTAs use action verbs that are informational or exploratory ('Read', 'Browse', 'Explore', 'Access') — never transactional or high-pressure ('Buy', 'Sign Up', 'Get Instant Access').", "Repeat key brand phrases across pages to build a consistent vocabulary ('utility engine', 'design with authority', 'serious game designers', 'the why not just the what').", "When listing tools or features, use a consistent three-part structure: [name] + [what it does] + [why it matters for design]. Do not vary this pattern within a list.", "Use 'From X to Y' constructions to establish the breadth of coverage without enumerating every item.", "Avoid hedging language ('might', 'could', 'perhaps', 'we think'). All statements are made with declarative confidence.", "Technical output formats (Unity, Unreal, XP tables) should be named explicitly to signal professional-grade applicability.", "Never use exclamation marks. Enthusiasm is conveyed through precision and directness, not punctuation."], "sentence_style": {"rhythm": "Staccato and purposeful — short declarative sentences are followed by slightly longer elaborative sentences, creating a push-pull rhythm. The cadence mimics a confident verbal briefing: assertion, then context, then implication. Lists of three items appear frequently, reinforcing a structured, methodical voice.", "connectives": "Minimal connective tissue between sentences — ideas are juxtaposed rather than linked with transitional phrases. 'And' is occasionally used mid-sentence. 'From... to...' constructions are used to establish range and scope. Paragraphs do not open with 'However', 'Therefore', or other formal academic connectives.", "average_length": "Short to medium — typically 10–20 words per sentence. Sentences are rarely compound-complex. Many key statements are deliberately short (under 10 words) for impact.", "structure_patterns": "Declarative statements dominate. Imperative constructions are used for commands and CTAs. The site frequently uses the pattern: [bold claim] + [supporting elaboration in the next sentence]. Fragments are used intentionally for rhetorical punch ('No installs, no data stored — just the numbers'). Em dashes are used to pivot from setup to payoff within a single sentence."}, "paragraph_style": {"structure": "Lead with the core claim or function, then add one or two sentences of elaboration or specification. No throat-clearing or contextual preamble — the first sentence is always the most important.", "typical_length": "2–4 sentences. Rarely longer. Single-sentence paragraphs appear in hero sections for maximum impact.", "opening_patterns": "Paragraphs frequently open with an imperative ('Stop using spreadsheets'), a declarative reframe ('Health is a lie'), a capability statement ('Access a suite of...'), or a direct noun phrase naming the subject ('A macro-economic sandbox for...')."}, "compliance_rules": [], "persuasion_approach": {"method": "The site persuades by diagnosing a problem the reader may not have named ('Stop guessing', 'Health is a lie', 'Stop using spreadsheets') and then positioning its tools as the logical, precise solution. It does not use testimonials, case studies, or emotional appeals — it persuades through demonstrated technical credibility and the implicit promise of professional-grade output.", "trust_building": "Trust is built through technical specificity (naming exact algorithms, simulation types, and output formats like Unity/Unreal compatibility), through transparency about what the tools do and don't do ('no installs, no data stored'), and through the consistent framing of the site as a utility rather than a product — something built for the work, not for the sale.", "social_proof_style": "Social proof is entirely absent from the provided content. No testimonials, user counts, client names, or community references appear. The site relies entirely on the implied authority of its technical depth."}}	adoption	site-adoption-agent	\N	Adopted from gamedesign.uk	f	2026-04-02 13:56:04.243032+00	2026-04-02 16:58:22.594724+00	site-adoption-agent	f
6c5a296d-27d3-4ef7-b889-f8b67347b498	1368e337-dd1d-4799-bbb3-8221a1b79bcc	briefing	{"tagline": "From idea to AI system", "about_us": "  Finetune is a small technical company focused on practical AI deployment.  We build systems that combine automation, data pipelines, model training, and agent-based architectures.  Our aim is to make AI genuinely useful.   ## AI systems that actually do the work  Most companies experimenting with AI end up with prototypes, demos, or chatbots.  Very few end up with systems that **save time, automate work, or generate measurable value**.  Finetune helps organisations design and deploy practical AI systems — automation pipelines, specialised models, and intelligent agents that solve real operational problems.  Not AI theatre.  ---  Real systems.  # Rapid experimentation  AI technology is moving quickly.  The companies that benefit most are those that can **experiment cheaply and deploy quickly**.  We aim to make it easy to test ideas, build prototypes, and evolve them into real systems when they prove useful.   ---  # What we build  We work with companies to build AI infrastructure that fits into real workflows.  ### Automation systems  AI that screens requests, processes documents, extracts information, and routes work automatically.  ### Intelligent agent pipelines  Systems where multiple AI agents collaborate to solve problems, analyse information, or complete complex tasks.  ### Custom AI models  Fine-tuned models trained on domain-specific data using techniques such as LoRA and other efficient training methods.  ### RAG systems for company knowledge  AI that can search and reason over internal data such as documents, emails, financial information, and knowledge bases.  ### Private AI deployments  Secure systems where models can run locally or within controlled environments to protect sensitive data.  ### Data collection infrastructure  Large-scale web data pipelines capable of gathering structured information from thousands of sources.  ---  # Why most AI projects fail  Many AI initiatives stall for predictable reasons.  **They start with tools instead of problems.**  **They stay in prototype mode.**  **They rely on third-party systems that can’t integrate with existing workflows.**  **Or they assume a single AI model will magically solve everything.**  In practice, useful AI systems usually require:  * data pipelines * automation layers * model adaptation * monitoring and iteration  AI works best as **part of a system**, not as a standalone tool.  ---  # The Finetune approach  We focus on **practical deployment**.  That means starting with a real operational problem and designing a system around it.  A typical project might involve:  1. Collecting and structuring the relevant data 2. Building automation pipelines 3. Training or adapting models 4. Integrating the system into existing workflows 5. Iterating until the system reliably saves time or improves decisions  The goal is not simply to use AI.  The goal is to make something **genuinely useful**.  ---  # What we can help with  Examples of problems we help solve:  * Screening and routing incoming requests * Automating quoting or proposal workflows * Extracting structured information from documents * Building internal knowledge assistants * Collecting market or financial data at scale * Deploying private AI models for sensitive workflows * Setting up agent pipelines that can perform complex multi-step tasks  Some systems are simple and highly targeted.  Others involve networks of cooperating agents and large-scale data pipelines.  ---  # A note on scale  We are particularly interested in **agent systems and large-scale automation**.  This includes experimentation with environments where thousands of AI agents can communicate and collaborate to solve problems.  These systems are still emerging, but they point toward a future where much of the routine work inside organisations can be handled by coordinated software agents.  Our goal is to help companies begin exploring that future in a practical way.  ---    ---  # Who we work with  We tend to work best with organisations that:  * have complex workflows or data * are open to experimentation * want to build internal capability with AI rather than just outsource it  This includes startups, established companies, and technical teams exploring new ways to operate.  ---  # About Finetune  Finetune is a small technical company focused on practical AI deployment.  We build systems that combine automation, data pipelines, model training, and agent-based architectures.  Our focus is simple:  **make AI genuinely useful inside real organisations.**  ---  # Call to action  ## Curious what AI could automate in your business?  We’re always happy to have a technical conversation about possible approaches.  Contact us to discuss your workflows, data, or ideas.", "services": [{"name": "AI Automation Systems  ", "description": "Design and deployment of AI-driven automation pipelines that remove manual work from business processes. Examples include request screening, automated quoting, document analysis, and workflow orchestration."}, {"name": "Intelligent Agent Systems", "description": "Design and deployment of networks of AI agents capable of solving complex tasks collaboratively. These systems can analyse information, communicate with each other, and perform large-scale problem solving. Talk to us about systems capable of running tens of thousands of AI agents communicating with each other to solve complex tasks."}, {"name": "Custom AI Model Training", "description": "Training specialised models using techniques such as LoRA, DoRA, and other parameter-efficient methods to adapt modern AI models to your company's specific needs."}, {"name": "RAG Systems for Company Data", "description": "Deployment of Retrieval-Augmented Generation systems that allow AI to search and reason over your internal data including:  Emails  Documents  Financial records  Marketing material  Knowledge bases"}, {"name": "Bulk Data Collection", "description": "Large-scale web data extraction across thousands of websites, including structured business and financial data collection."}, {"name": "Financial and Company Data Pipelines", "description": "Automated systems for collecting and analysing financial data from public sources including company registries and regulatory filings."}, {"name": "Private AI Deployments", "description": "Secure deployment of AI systems that run privately — including models that can run locally or in-browser to protect company data and reduce usage costs."}, {"name": "Agent Pipeline Infrastructure", "description": "Installation and configuration of agent frameworks and orchestration systems so companies can run their own AI agents in production."}, {"name": "Rapid Website Generation", "description": "Creation of complex, high-quality static websites within minutes for:  company identity  marketing pages  landing pages  documentation  presentations"}, {"name": "AI Strategy & Implementation", "description": "Hands-on support helping companies move from curiosity about AI to systems that actually work inside their business."}, {"name": "High-Volume Data Acquisition", "description": "Design systems capable of collecting structured information from thousands of websites automatically."}], "company_name": "FineTuning"}	pageflow-builder	briefing-agent	\N	\N	f	2026-03-21 11:56:53.963921+00	2026-03-21 13:14:46.385511+00	briefing-agent	f
009004d7-b88a-408c-b941-e15881777c2a	4851f6fc-71cf-4160-a270-e03d6d3e0732	identity	{"tone": "professional yet approachable", "email": "leopardess@contactforsales.com", "phone": "+44 (0) 7934 524 911", "tagline": "Agile AI Agents: Move Swiftly into Digital Transformation with Grace and Precision", "industry": "AI consulting / agent orchestration / technology services", "services": [{"name": "Automated Website Solutions", "description": ""}, {"name": "Multi-Agent Systems", "description": ""}, {"name": "Digital Transformation Strategy", "description": ""}, {"name": "Web Consultancy", "description": ""}], "site_brief": "Industry: AI consulting and agent orchestration (technology services). Primary audience: Three personas — (1) CTOs and engineering leads at UK scale-ups (50–500 employees) who need proof of production deployments and working code, not slide decks; (2) Founders of B2B SaaS or professional services firms who are technically literate, lack an internal AI/ML team, and need a delivery partner who can own end-to-end build; (3) Operations and transformation leads at mid-market UK businesses seeking practical automation of high-volume workflows without hiring a full data engineering team. All three personas are time-poor, results-driven, and respond to specificity, working demos, and clear ROI framing. Tone: Practical, direct, technically credible — the voice of engineers who build real systems, not consultants who produce reports. Conversion purpose: Generate qualified inbound leads and demo requests from UK businesses ready to commission AI agent or automation work, with a secondary goal of establishing technical authority through case studies, tools, and insights content.", "company_name": "Leopardess Consulting", "about_summary": "At Leopardess Consulting, we like building smart, hierarchical AI agent systems that help businesses adapt quickly and effectively. Using tools like Kubernetes for orchestration, Kafka for smooth messaging, and Postgres for reliable storage, we create solutions that automate complex workflows – think spinning up and launching full multipage websites on Cloudflare in no time. We're here to help you make the most of AI with practical, dependable approaches that suit your team.", "leadership_team": [{"bio": "Peter is a technology strategist with strong enthusiasm for AI agent frameworks, modern web architecture, and automated systems. He leads our efforts in digital transformation, delivering agile innovation through effective multi-agent orchestration and quick deployments.", "name": "Peter Grenfell", "title": "Founder & Principal Consultant"}], "target_audience": "Primary: CTOs, engineering leads, and technical founders at UK scale-ups and B2B SaaS companies who evaluate vendors on production credibility, stack depth, and delivery track record. Secondary: technically-literate business decision-makers capable of assessing system architecture and engineering quality. Non-technical SMB buyers are explicitly out of scope. All pages should be written for the primary audience by default. Benefit-led or simplified language is only appropriate when used to frame a technical outcome, not as a substitute for technical specificity. Pages that currently address non-technical readers (e.g. about page) should be revised to restore technical register consistency with the index page.", "key_differentiators": "Our agile approach to AI orchestration\\nFast deployments (minutes instead of weeks)\\nSolid enterprise-level security and reliability\\nProven stack with Kubernetes, Kafka, and Postgres\\nStrong expertise in hierarchical multi-agent systems\\nSpecial focus on web automation – with room to grow into other areas", "brand_voice_guidelines": "The Leopardess name is a live brand asset and should be referenced naturally at least once per page through metaphor rather than explicit explanation. The leopardess archetype carries: speed (fast delivery, rapid iteration, no slow consulting cycles), precision (targeted solutions, no wasted motion, surgical automation), adaptability (hunts in complex terrain, works across stacks and sectors), and instinct (pattern recognition, knowing where to move before the client does). Apply these qualities to describe how the team works, how systems are built, and how problems are approached. Examples: 'We move fast because we've already mapped the terrain', 'precision-built agents, not broad-brush automation', 'we don't advise from the sidelines — we hunt the problem alongside you'. Avoid forced or literal animal references. The metaphor should feel like a natural extension of the practical-expert voice, not a mascot gimmick.", "industry_audience_tone_purpose": "Industry: AI consulting and agent orchestration / technology services. Target Audience: CTOs and engineering leads at UK scale-ups (50–500 employees) who need proof of production deployments; founders of B2B SaaS or professional services companies evaluating build-vs-buy for agent orchestration; operations and transformation leads at mid-market UK businesses automating high-volume workflows. Tone: Direct, technically credible, evidence-led — the voice of practitioners who ship working systems, not consultants who produce decks. Purpose: Lead generation and service conversion, achieved by demonstrating technical depth, showing real production deployments, and giving time-poor technical buyers enough specificity to self-qualify and initiate contact."}	content-gap-planner	content-gap-planner	\N	\N	f	2026-03-15 01:39:12.730718+00	2026-03-15 01:57:51.414732+00	content-gap-planner	f
3e3f0ac8-9eee-4e6d-aba2-3e86e5202d4b	e1e22a7d-0552-405a-85b3-1b1e51384df5	mission	{"mission": "AI-driven social platform where the world is the content and your take is the game", "tagline": "TikTok is where you watch. Twitter is where you shout. Spark is where you play.", "positioning": "Not another social app. A daily game powered by what is happening in the world. You don't post. You don't scroll. You play.", "content_tone": {"is": ["interesting", "slightly adult", "competitive", "aspirational", "playful", "game-like", "energetic"], "is_not": ["dark", "gruesome", "violent", "xxx", "newspaper news", "corporate", "generic social media", "AI slop"], "ai_role": "Producer not performer. AI frames, scores, curates, synthesises. Never does humour, hot takes, sarcasm."}, "target_users": {"primary": "People with opinions on current trends but no platform that asks for them", "tertiary": "Curators and tastemakers — people who find things, not make things", "secondary": "Creators wanting skill showcase with built-in audience and niche discovery"}, "core_concepts": {"cold_start": "Solo mode fully functional for 1 person. AI sparring partner fills the room transparently.", "engage_first": "No signup walls, no explanations. A provocation card and a text input. Engage first, understand second, commit third.", "archetype_system": "Profile emerges from behaviour. Daily Gauntlet produces shareable archetype cards.", "ai_slop_awareness": "AI restricted to genuine strengths. Deliberately bad AI takes as challenge seeds.", "the_game_metaphor": "Everything is framed as a game. Challenges have timers, rules, scores, winners."}, "key_differentiators": ["AI as game master and producer, never the performer", "Opinion-first entry point — you need a take, not an idea", "Ephemeral challenges with permanent emergent reputation", "Rooms not feeds — enter a space with energy, don't scroll a stream", "Arena (competitive takes) + Stage (creative showcase) dual modes", "Archetypes earned from behaviour, not self-reported"], "measurable_objectives": [{"id": "obj_dau_50", "metric": "daily_active_users", "target": 50, "objective": "50 DAU within 4 weeks"}, {"id": "obj_gauntlet_rate", "metric": "gauntlet_completion_rate", "target": 0.15, "objective": "Gauntlet completion >15% of visitors"}, {"id": "obj_session_duration", "metric": "avg_session_duration_seconds", "target": 180, "objective": "Avg session >3 minutes"}, {"id": "obj_share_rate", "metric": "gauntlet_share_rate", "target": 0.05, "objective": ">5% Gauntlet completions shared"}]}	domain-submitter	domain-submitter	\N	\N	t	2026-04-02 09:49:00.960158+00	\N	domain-submitter	f
a75d2294-280a-432c-a79c-32bcf0cc6ed7	4851f6fc-71cf-4160-a270-e03d6d3e0732	voice	{"tone_and_register": "All copy must maintain a single consistent register: technically precise, direct, and credible. Brand language ('Grace and Precision', 'Leopardess') is permitted as a framing device but must always resolve into a concrete technical or delivery claim within the same sentence or paragraph. Brand metaphors must never stand alone or substitute for specificity. The pattern is: brand framing → technical substance, not brand framing → benefit language. Example: 'Grace and Precision means your orchestration layer handles failover without manual intervention — not that we care deeply about your outcomes.' Aspirational or poetic phrasing that is not immediately grounded in a system behaviour, stack component, or delivery outcome should be removed or rewritten. This applies to all pages including index, about, and services."}	content-gap-planner	content-gap-planner	\N	\N	f	2026-03-15 01:40:19.285326+00	2026-03-15 02:09:21.692388+00	content-gap-planner	f
