6e5d152c-1fb4-449c-bfa0-d1eb0f926343	15a6cb16-5a86-4541-a8e4-d7106239b6a4	content_direction	{"voice": {"person": "Second person ('you') is used throughout for direct address and to place the reader in an active role; third person is used only when describing tools or concepts as objects ('Health is a lie'); first person is entirely absent, keeping the brand authoritative and impersonal rather than personal or conversational.", "register": "Professional-technical with a sharp, commanding edge — the voice of a senior practitioner who has earned the right to give direct instruction and expects the reader to keep up, without being condescending or academic.", "formality": "Sits at the formal-technical end of the spectrum but avoids academic stuffiness. Contractions are absent. Sentences are tight and declarative. Jargon is used freely but always purposefully, never as decoration. The register is closer to a senior engineer's documentation than a blog post.", "emotional_tone": "Challenging and galvanising — the tone implies the reader has been doing things the wrong way (guessing, using spreadsheets, misunderstanding health mechanics) and positions the site as the corrective. It is not warm or reassuring; it respects the reader by being direct and slightly provocative.", "authority_level": "Authority is established through demonstrated technical specificity — naming exact algorithms (Monte Carlo, Lanchester), exact output formats (Unity, Unreal), and exact design domains (EHP, TTK, XP curves) — rather than through credentials, testimonials, or claims of experience. The site earns trust by showing it knows the work intimately."}, "formatted": "Sentence style:\\nAverage length: Short to medium — typically 8 to 18 words per sentence. Declarative statements dominate. Fragment sentences are used deliberately for impact and rhythm, particularly in headlines and opening lines.\\nStructure patterns: Sentences follow a Subject-Verb-Object pattern with minimal subordinate clauses. Imperative constructions are used for commands ('Stop guessing. Start calculating.'). Noun-heavy descriptive sentences are used for feature descriptions ('A macro-economic sandbox for MMO and live-service designers.'). Fragments are used as punchy standalone statements ('Health is a lie.').\\nRhythm: The writing has a staccato, punchy cadence — short declarative sentences are followed by slightly longer explanatory ones. Pairs and triplets of short sentences create momentum. The rhythm accelerates with imperative fragments and decelerates with technical specification lists.\\nConnectives: Connectives are minimal. Ideas are juxtaposed rather than linked with transitional language. Dashes (—) are used frequently as connective punctuation to append clarifying or intensifying clauses. 'And' is the only conjunction used with any frequency; 'however', 'therefore', 'furthermore' are absent.\\n\\nPersuasion approach:\\nMethod: The site persuades through reframing — it identifies a flawed assumption the reader holds ('Health is a lie', 'Stop using spreadsheets') and immediately offers the correct framework. This creates intellectual buy-in before any product is mentioned. The persuasion is problem-first, solution-second, with no soft sell or emotional appeal.\\nTrust building: Trust is built entirely through demonstrated domain expertise. The site uses precise technical vocabulary (Monte Carlo simulations, Lanchester combat models, EHP, CPI) without definition, signalling that it is written by and for practitioners. The promise 'no setup required' and 'no data stored' builds operational trust through transparency about constraints.\\nSocial proof style: Social proof is entirely absent from the provided content. No testimonials, case studies, user counts, or named clients appear. The site relies solely on the authority of its content and technical specificity to establish credibility.\\n\\nContent depth:\\nThoroughness: Each feature or section is described at the level of purpose and mechanism — enough to demonstrate genuine expertise and communicate value, but not so deep as to replace the tool or guide itself. Descriptions are 2-4 sentences: one sentence naming the problem, one naming the solution, one specifying the technical capability.\\nAssumed knowledge: The reader is assumed to be a working game designer or developer with existing familiarity with concepts like XP curves, TTK, EHP, MMO economy design, Monte Carlo methods, and game engines (Unity, Unreal). No foundational definitions are provided. The reader is expected to understand why these tools matter without being told.\\nExplanation pattern: Complex ideas are introduced by first naming the misconception or limitation ('Stop using spreadsheets for leveling curves'), then naming the correct approach ('Design, visualise, and export XP tables using Linear, Exponential, or Polynomial algorithms'), then specifying the output or application ('with output ready for direct use in Unity or Unreal'). The pattern is: challenge assumption → name solution → specify capability.\\n\\nWriting rules:\\n- Open sections with a short imperative or declarative fragment that challenges an assumption or reframes the reader's understanding before any explanation is given.\\n- Use pairs of contrasting imperatives to create momentum and define the value proposition: 'Stop guessing. Start calculating.' — always negative command followed by positive command.\\n- Never use first person. The brand has no 'I' or 'we'. All statements are either directed at 'you' or describe tools and concepts in the third person.\\n- Use the em dash (—) to append a clarifying or intensifying clause to a sentence rather than starting a new sentence or using a comma. This is the primary connective device.\\n- Technical terms (Monte Carlo, EHP, TTK, Lanchester, CPI) are used without definition or apology. Never explain what a term means if the target reader can be assumed to know it.\\n- Feature descriptions follow a three-part structure: name the problem or limitation the reader currently has, name the solution the tool provides, specify the technical output or application.\\n- Paragraphs are 2-4 sentences maximum. One concept per paragraph. Never pad with transitional filler.\\n- Headings at the section level are short declarative statements or imperative commands, not questions. Subheadings name the tool or concept directly without decoration.\\n- Avoid superlatives, hyperbole, and unverifiable claims. Do not write 'the best', 'revolutionary', 'industry-leading', or 'powerful'. Let technical specificity carry the authority.\\n- Calls-to-action use active, directional verbs that describe what the reader will do, not what they will receive: 'Read', 'Browse', 'Explore', 'Access'. Never 'Get', 'Discover', or 'Unlock'.\\n- When describing a tool's output, name the specific format or application context (e.g., 'output ready for direct use in Unity or Unreal') to make the value concrete and practical.\\n- Use sentence fragments as standalone statements for maximum impact, particularly in hero sections and feature introductions. 'Health is a lie.' is a complete unit of content.\\n- Lists of tools or capabilities should use parallel grammatical structure — each item follows the same syntactic pattern (noun phrase, imperative, or verb-noun construction).\\n- The phrase 'serious game designers' or equivalent ('designers who want to understand the why') should be used to define and qualify the intended audience, implicitly excluding casual users.\\n- Avoid filler phrases like 'in order to', 'as well as', 'a wide range of', 'we are pleased to offer'. Every word must carry functional weight.\\n\\nHeading style:\\nFormat: H1 headings are short, commanding statements or imperative fragments — often 3-6 words, provocative, and designed to reframe the reader's current approach. H2 headings are slightly longer declarative statements that describe what the section delivers. H3 headings are direct noun phrases naming the tool or concept without decoration.\\nHierarchy: H1 is used once per page as the primary value statement or challenge. H2 introduces major content sections with a descriptive promise. H3 labels individual tools, features, or subsections by name. The hierarchy moves from provocative challenge (H1) to contextual promise (H2) to specific item (H3).\\nExamples from site:\\n- Design with Authority.\\n- Open on Your Second Monitor. Built for the Work.\\n- The 'Why' Behind the 'What'\\n- Everything You Need to Design Better Games\\n- Stop Guessing. Start Calculating.\\n\\nVoice:\\nRegister: Professional-technical with a sharp, commanding edge — the voice of a senior practitioner who has earned the right to give direct instruction and expects the reader to keep up, without being condescending or academic.\\nPerson: Second person ('you') is used throughout for direct address and to place the reader in an active role; third person is used only when describing tools or concepts as objects ('Health is a lie'); first person is entirely absent, keeping the brand authoritative and impersonal rather than personal or conversational.\\nAuthority level: Authority is established through demonstrated technical specificity — naming exact algorithms (Monte Carlo, Lanchester), exact output formats (Unity, Unreal), and exact design domains (EHP, TTK, XP curves) — rather than through credentials, testimonials, or claims of experience. The site earns trust by showing it knows the work intimately.\\nEmotional tone: Challenging and galvanising — the tone implies the reader has been doing things the wrong way (guessing, using spreadsheets, misunderstanding health mechanics) and positions the site as the corrective. It is not warm or reassuring; it respects the reader by being direct and slightly provocative.\\nFormality: Sits at the formal-technical end of the spectrum but avoids academic stuffiness. Contractions are absent. Sentences are tight and declarative. Jargon is used freely but always purposefully, never as decoration. The register is closer to a senior engineer's documentation than a blog post.", "content_depth": {"thoroughness": "Each feature or section is described at the level of purpose and mechanism — enough to demonstrate genuine expertise and communicate value, but not so deep as to replace the tool or guide itself. Descriptions are 2-4 sentences: one sentence naming the problem, one naming the solution, one specifying the technical capability.", "assumed_knowledge": "The reader is assumed to be a working game designer or developer with existing familiarity with concepts like XP curves, TTK, EHP, MMO economy design, Monte Carlo methods, and game engines (Unity, Unreal). No foundational definitions are provided. The reader is expected to understand why these tools matter without being told.", "explanation_pattern": "Complex ideas are introduced by first naming the misconception or limitation ('Stop using spreadsheets for leveling curves'), then naming the correct approach ('Design, visualise, and export XP tables using Linear, Exponential, or Polynomial algorithms'), then specifying the output or application ('with output ready for direct use in Unity or Unreal'). The pattern is: challenge assumption → name solution → specify capability."}, "heading_style": {"format": "H1 headings are short, commanding statements or imperative fragments — often 3-6 words, provocative, and designed to reframe the reader's current approach. H2 headings are slightly longer declarative statements that describe what the section delivers. H3 headings are direct noun phrases naming the tool or concept without decoration.", "hierarchy": "H1 is used once per page as the primary value statement or challenge. H2 introduces major content sections with a descriptive promise. H3 labels individual tools, features, or subsections by name. The hierarchy moves from provocative challenge (H1) to contextual promise (H2) to specific item (H3).", "examples_from_site": ["Design with Authority.", "Open on Your Second Monitor. Built for the Work.", "The 'Why' Behind the 'What'", "Everything You Need to Design Better Games", "Stop Guessing. Start Calculating."]}, "writing_rules": ["Open sections with a short imperative or declarative fragment that challenges an assumption or reframes the reader's understanding before any explanation is given.", "Use pairs of contrasting imperatives to create momentum and define the value proposition: 'Stop guessing. Start calculating.' — always negative command followed by positive command.", "Never use first person. The brand has no 'I' or 'we'. All statements are either directed at 'you' or describe tools and concepts in the third person.", "Use the em dash (—) to append a clarifying or intensifying clause to a sentence rather than starting a new sentence or using a comma. This is the primary connective device.", "Technical terms (Monte Carlo, EHP, TTK, Lanchester, CPI) are used without definition or apology. Never explain what a term means if the target reader can be assumed to know it.", "Feature descriptions follow a three-part structure: name the problem or limitation the reader currently has, name the solution the tool provides, specify the technical output or application.", "Paragraphs are 2-4 sentences maximum. One concept per paragraph. Never pad with transitional filler.", "Headings at the section level are short declarative statements or imperative commands, not questions. Subheadings name the tool or concept directly without decoration.", "Avoid superlatives, hyperbole, and unverifiable claims. Do not write 'the best', 'revolutionary', 'industry-leading', or 'powerful'. Let technical specificity carry the authority.", "Calls-to-action use active, directional verbs that describe what the reader will do, not what they will receive: 'Read', 'Browse', 'Explore', 'Access'. Never 'Get', 'Discover', or 'Unlock'.", "When describing a tool's output, name the specific format or application context (e.g., 'output ready for direct use in Unity or Unreal') to make the value concrete and practical.", "Use sentence fragments as standalone statements for maximum impact, particularly in hero sections and feature introductions. 'Health is a lie.' is a complete unit of content.", "Lists of tools or capabilities should use parallel grammatical structure — each item follows the same syntactic pattern (noun phrase, imperative, or verb-noun construction).", "The phrase 'serious game designers' or equivalent ('designers who want to understand the why') should be used to define and qualify the intended audience, implicitly excluding casual users.", "Avoid filler phrases like 'in order to', 'as well as', 'a wide range of', 'we are pleased to offer'. Every word must carry functional weight."], "sentence_style": {"rhythm": "The writing has a staccato, punchy cadence — short declarative sentences are followed by slightly longer explanatory ones. Pairs and triplets of short sentences create momentum. The rhythm accelerates with imperative fragments and decelerates with technical specification lists.", "connectives": "Connectives are minimal. Ideas are juxtaposed rather than linked with transitional language. Dashes (—) are used frequently as connective punctuation to append clarifying or intensifying clauses. 'And' is the only conjunction used with any frequency; 'however', 'therefore', 'furthermore' are absent.", "average_length": "Short to medium — typically 8 to 18 words per sentence. Declarative statements dominate. Fragment sentences are used deliberately for impact and rhythm, particularly in headlines and opening lines.", "structure_patterns": "Sentences follow a Subject-Verb-Object pattern with minimal subordinate clauses. Imperative constructions are used for commands ('Stop guessing. Start calculating.'). Noun-heavy descriptive sentences are used for feature descriptions ('A macro-economic sandbox for MMO and live-service designers.'). Fragments are used as punchy standalone statements ('Health is a lie.')."}, "compliance_rules": [], "persuasion_approach": {"method": "The site persuades through reframing — it identifies a flawed assumption the reader holds ('Health is a lie', 'Stop using spreadsheets') and immediately offers the correct framework. This creates intellectual buy-in before any product is mentioned. The persuasion is problem-first, solution-second, with no soft sell or emotional appeal.", "trust_building": "Trust is built entirely through demonstrated domain expertise. The site uses precise technical vocabulary (Monte Carlo simulations, Lanchester combat models, EHP, CPI) without definition, signalling that it is written by and for practitioners. The promise 'no setup required' and 'no data stored' builds operational trust through transparency about constraints.", "social_proof_style": "Social proof is entirely absent from the provided content. No testimonials, case studies, user counts, or named clients appear. The site relies solely on the authority of its content and technical specificity to establish credibility."}}	adoption	site-adoption-agent	\N	Adopted from gamedesign.uk	f	2026-04-02 16:58:22.594724+00	2026-04-03 08:51:48.969225+00	site-adoption-agent	f
009559f8-cf8f-4bf6-b138-840069e86bfe	15a6cb16-5a86-4541-a8e4-d7106239b6a4	content_direction	{"voice": {"person": "Second person ('you') is used consistently in CTAs and value propositions to create direct, personal engagement; third person is used when describing tools, systems, and concepts objectively; first person is entirely absent, maintaining an authoritative, product-focused stance rather than a personal or editorial one.", "register": "Professional-technical with a sharp, confident edge — the voice of a senior practitioner who has earned the right to be direct, speaks to peers rather than students, and treats the reader as an intelligent professional who values precision over hand-holding.", "formality": "Sits at the formal-technical end of the spectrum but with deliberate moments of punchy informality — short declarative fragments, rhetorical provocations like 'Health is a lie' — that prevent it feeling academic or sterile; no slang, no exclamation marks, no casual filler phrases.", "emotional_tone": "Challenging and empowering simultaneously — the writing implies the reader has been doing things the wrong way (guessing, using spreadsheets) and positions the site as the corrective force, creating a mild productive tension that motivates action without being condescending.", "authority_level": "Authority is established through demonstrated technical specificity — naming exact methodologies (Monte Carlo, Lanchester combat models, EHP calculations), specific algorithms (Linear, Exponential, Polynomial), and real engine ecosystems (Unity, Unreal) — never through claimed credentials, testimonials, or self-description as 'expert' or 'leading'."}, "formatted": "Heading style:\\nFormat: Headings are either bold declarative statements, imperative commands, or provocative fragments — never questions, never 'How to' constructions, never gerund-led phrases; they are short (2-7 words typically) and written to stand alone as complete thoughts without needing the body copy to make sense.\\nHierarchy: H1 is used for the single dominant positioning statement of the page — it is the boldest, most compressed expression of the page's purpose; H2 introduces major thematic sections; H3 labels individual tools, guides, or features within those sections; hierarchy is functional and consistent, not decorative.\\nExamples from site:\\n- Design with Authority.\\n- The 'Why' Behind the 'What'\\n- Open on Your Second Monitor. Built for the Work.\\n- Stop Guessing. Start Calculating.\\n- Everything You Need to Design Better Games\\n\\nParagraph style:\\nTypical length: 2-3 sentences in feature/tool descriptions; occasionally a single sentence for maximum impact in positioning statements; never more than 4 sentences in a single block.\\nStructure: Paragraphs lead with the core claim or function, then support with a specific technical detail or use-case example; the final sentence either reinforces the value or names the output/outcome.\\nOpening patterns: Paragraphs frequently open with a noun phrase naming the subject directly ('Health is a lie.', 'A macro-economic sandbox for...', 'Deep dives into the theory...') rather than transitional phrases; some open with an imperative that reframes the reader's current behaviour before offering the solution.\\n\\nVoice:\\nRegister: Professional-technical with a sharp, confident edge — the voice of a senior practitioner who has earned the right to be direct, speaks to peers rather than students, and treats the reader as an intelligent professional who values precision over hand-holding.\\nPerson: Second person ('you') is used consistently in CTAs and value propositions to create direct, personal engagement; third person is used when describing tools, systems, and concepts objectively; first person is entirely absent, maintaining an authoritative, product-focused stance rather than a personal or editorial one.\\nAuthority level: Authority is established through demonstrated technical specificity — naming exact methodologies (Monte Carlo, Lanchester combat models, EHP calculations), specific algorithms (Linear, Exponential, Polynomial), and real engine ecosystems (Unity, Unreal) — never through claimed credentials, testimonials, or self-description as 'expert' or 'leading'.\\nEmotional tone: Challenging and empowering simultaneously — the writing implies the reader has been doing things the wrong way (guessing, using spreadsheets) and positions the site as the corrective force, creating a mild productive tension that motivates action without being condescending.\\nFormality: Sits at the formal-technical end of the spectrum but with deliberate moments of punchy informality — short declarative fragments, rhetorical provocations like 'Health is a lie' — that prevent it feeling academic or sterile; no slang, no exclamation marks, no casual filler phrases.\\n\\nSentence style:\\nAverage length: Short to medium; most sentences run 10-20 words, with frequent use of very short declarative fragments (3-7 words) for emphasis, particularly in headings and opening statements.\\nStructure patterns: Favours subject-verb-object simplicity with minimal subordinate clauses; uses paired imperative constructions ('Stop guessing. Start calculating.'); opens sections with a bold claim or provocation, then follows with a longer explanatory sentence that delivers the substance; avoids passive voice almost entirely.\\nRhythm: Staccato and punchy at the macro level — short sentences create beats — then expands into denser, noun-heavy technical description when listing capabilities; the rhythm alternates between 'hook' (short, bold) and 'delivery' (medium, specific) within each content block.\\nConnectives: Minimal use of transitional connectives; ideas are juxtaposed rather than bridged with 'however', 'therefore', or 'additionally'; em-dashes are used to append clarifying phrases without softening the primary statement; lists replace connective prose when enumerating features.\\n\\nPersuasion approach:\\nMethod: Persuasion through demonstrated utility and implicit problem-identification — the site names the pain (guessing, spreadsheets, not understanding the 'why') and immediately presents the functional solution without emotional manipulation, testimonials, or urgency tactics; the argument is 'here is a precise tool for a real problem you have'.\\nTrust building: Trust is built exclusively through technical specificity and the absence of vague marketing language; naming exact simulation types, algorithms, and output formats (e.g., 'output ready for direct use in Unity or Unreal') signals that the creators have deep domain knowledge and have thought through practical workflow integration.\\nSocial proof style: Social proof is entirely absent from the observed content — no testimonials, no user counts, no logos, no case studies; credibility is derived from the quality and specificity of the content itself rather than external validation.\\n\\nContent depth:\\nThoroughness: Section-level content is deliberately concise — each tool or guide is described in 2-4 sentences that capture purpose, mechanism, and value without exhaustive detail; the implication is that depth lives inside the tools and guides themselves, not in the marketing copy describing them.\\nAssumed knowledge: The reader is assumed to be an intermediate-to-advanced game designer or developer who already knows what TTK, EHP, Monte Carlo simulation, Lanchester models, and XP curves are; terms are named without definition, treating them as shared vocabulary rather than concepts requiring introduction.\\nExplanation pattern: Complex ideas are introduced by naming the concept directly, then immediately contextualising its practical application in game design; no analogies to non-gaming domains are used; explanations move from the abstract label to the concrete use case in a single sentence.\\n\\nWriting rules:\\n- Open every major section with a short, declarative or imperative statement of 10 words or fewer — this is the hook, not the explanation.\\n- Follow every short hook sentence with a longer sentence (15-25 words) that delivers the specific, technical substance of the claim.\\n- Never use first person singular or plural ('I', 'we', 'our team') in body copy; the brand voice is the product, not the people behind it.\\n- Use second person ('you') only in CTAs and problem-identification statements, not in technical descriptions of tools.\\n- Technical terms (Monte Carlo, EHP, Lanchester, TTK) are used without definition or apology — assume the reader knows them or will look them up; do not parenthetically explain them.\\n- Paragraphs in descriptive sections are 2-4 sentences maximum; each paragraph covers exactly one tool, concept, or value proposition.\\n- CTAs use active, directional verbs ('Read', 'Browse', 'Explore', 'Access') and are never phrased as questions or soft suggestions.\\n- Avoid superlatives ('best', 'most powerful', 'ultimate') entirely; let specificity do the work that superlatives would normally do.\\n- Paired imperative constructions ('Stop X. Start Y.') are a signature structural device — use them for maximum-impact positioning statements, not for instructional steps.\\n- When listing tool capabilities, use noun phrases rather than full sentences to maintain a technical, spec-sheet register.\\n- Never use exclamation marks; confidence is expressed through declarative certainty, not punctuation-based enthusiasm.\\n- The em-dash is used to append a clarifying or expanding clause to a statement without creating a subordinate sentence — it maintains momentum while adding precision.\\n- Avoid hedging language ('might', 'could', 'perhaps', 'we think'); every statement is made with full commitment.\\n- Describe what a tool does mechanically before describing why it matters — function precedes benefit in technical copy.\\n- Use 'serious' as a qualifier for the target audience ('serious game designers') to signal that this is not beginner or hobbyist content.", "content_depth": {"thoroughness": "Section-level content is deliberately concise — each tool or guide is described in 2-4 sentences that capture purpose, mechanism, and value without exhaustive detail; the implication is that depth lives inside the tools and guides themselves, not in the marketing copy describing them.", "assumed_knowledge": "The reader is assumed to be an intermediate-to-advanced game designer or developer who already knows what TTK, EHP, Monte Carlo simulation, Lanchester models, and XP curves are; terms are named without definition, treating them as shared vocabulary rather than concepts requiring introduction.", "explanation_pattern": "Complex ideas are introduced by naming the concept directly, then immediately contextualising its practical application in game design; no analogies to non-gaming domains are used; explanations move from the abstract label to the concrete use case in a single sentence."}, "heading_style": {"format": "Headings are either bold declarative statements, imperative commands, or provocative fragments — never questions, never 'How to' constructions, never gerund-led phrases; they are short (2-7 words typically) and written to stand alone as complete thoughts without needing the body copy to make sense.", "hierarchy": "H1 is used for the single dominant positioning statement of the page — it is the boldest, most compressed expression of the page's purpose; H2 introduces major thematic sections; H3 labels individual tools, guides, or features within those sections; hierarchy is functional and consistent, not decorative.", "examples_from_site": ["Design with Authority.", "The 'Why' Behind the 'What'", "Open on Your Second Monitor. Built for the Work.", "Stop Guessing. Start Calculating.", "Everything You Need to Design Better Games"]}, "writing_rules": ["Open every major section with a short, declarative or imperative statement of 10 words or fewer — this is the hook, not the explanation.", "Follow every short hook sentence with a longer sentence (15-25 words) that delivers the specific, technical substance of the claim.", "Never use first person singular or plural ('I', 'we', 'our team') in body copy; the brand voice is the product, not the people behind it.", "Use second person ('you') only in CTAs and problem-identification statements, not in technical descriptions of tools.", "Technical terms (Monte Carlo, EHP, Lanchester, TTK) are used without definition or apology — assume the reader knows them or will look them up; do not parenthetically explain them.", "Paragraphs in descriptive sections are 2-4 sentences maximum; each paragraph covers exactly one tool, concept, or value proposition.", "CTAs use active, directional verbs ('Read', 'Browse', 'Explore', 'Access') and are never phrased as questions or soft suggestions.", "Avoid superlatives ('best', 'most powerful', 'ultimate') entirely; let specificity do the work that superlatives would normally do.", "Paired imperative constructions ('Stop X. Start Y.') are a signature structural device — use them for maximum-impact positioning statements, not for instructional steps.", "When listing tool capabilities, use noun phrases rather than full sentences to maintain a technical, spec-sheet register.", "Never use exclamation marks; confidence is expressed through declarative certainty, not punctuation-based enthusiasm.", "The em-dash is used to append a clarifying or expanding clause to a statement without creating a subordinate sentence — it maintains momentum while adding precision.", "Avoid hedging language ('might', 'could', 'perhaps', 'we think'); every statement is made with full commitment.", "Describe what a tool does mechanically before describing why it matters — function precedes benefit in technical copy.", "Use 'serious' as a qualifier for the target audience ('serious game designers') to signal that this is not beginner or hobbyist content."], "sentence_style": {"rhythm": "Staccato and punchy at the macro level — short sentences create beats — then expands into denser, noun-heavy technical description when listing capabilities; the rhythm alternates between 'hook' (short, bold) and 'delivery' (medium, specific) within each content block.", "connectives": "Minimal use of transitional connectives; ideas are juxtaposed rather than bridged with 'however', 'therefore', or 'additionally'; em-dashes are used to append clarifying phrases without softening the primary statement; lists replace connective prose when enumerating features.", "average_length": "Short to medium; most sentences run 10-20 words, with frequent use of very short declarative fragments (3-7 words) for emphasis, particularly in headings and opening statements.", "structure_patterns": "Favours subject-verb-object simplicity with minimal subordinate clauses; uses paired imperative constructions ('Stop guessing. Start calculating.'); opens sections with a bold claim or provocation, then follows with a longer explanatory sentence that delivers the substance; avoids passive voice almost entirely."}, "paragraph_style": {"structure": "Paragraphs lead with the core claim or function, then support with a specific technical detail or use-case example; the final sentence either reinforces the value or names the output/outcome.", "typical_length": "2-3 sentences in feature/tool descriptions; occasionally a single sentence for maximum impact in positioning statements; never more than 4 sentences in a single block.", "opening_patterns": "Paragraphs frequently open with a noun phrase naming the subject directly ('Health is a lie.', 'A macro-economic sandbox for...', 'Deep dives into the theory...') rather than transitional phrases; some open with an imperative that reframes the reader's current behaviour before offering the solution."}, "compliance_rules": [], "persuasion_approach": {"method": "Persuasion through demonstrated utility and implicit problem-identification — the site names the pain (guessing, spreadsheets, not understanding the 'why') and immediately presents the functional solution without emotional manipulation, testimonials, or urgency tactics; the argument is 'here is a precise tool for a real problem you have'.", "trust_building": "Trust is built exclusively through technical specificity and the absence of vague marketing language; naming exact simulation types, algorithms, and output formats (e.g., 'output ready for direct use in Unity or Unreal') signals that the creators have deep domain knowledge and have thought through practical workflow integration.", "social_proof_style": "Social proof is entirely absent from the observed content — no testimonials, no user counts, no logos, no case studies; credibility is derived from the quality and specificity of the content itself rather than external validation."}}	adoption	site-adoption-agent	\N	Adopted from gamedesign.uk	t	2026-04-03 08:51:48.969225+00	\N	site-adoption-agent	f
