 exclamation marks.", "site_metadata": "Tagline: Rigorous tools and theory for game designers who build real systems. Purpose: GameDesign.uk provides mathematically grounded tools, calculators, and practitioner-level guides for independent and mid-size studio game designers solving real balance, economy, and systems design problems. Target audience: Independent and mid-size studio game designers and systems designers past the beginner stage — comfortable with mathematics, building real games, managing live economies, and seeking rigorous directly applicable methodology over guesswork. Tone: Formal-technical with a sharp, confident edge — authoritative through demonstrated specificity, not claimed credentials; challenging and empowering; second person in CTAs, third person for systems and concepts, first person absent.", "target_audience": "Primary: Independent and mid-size studio game designers and systems designers who are past the beginner stage — building real games, managing real economies, and solving real balance problems. Comfortable with mathematics, frustrated by guesswork, seeking rigorous and directly applicable tools and theory. Secondary: Technically-minded game design students who are self-directed and seeking practitioner-level depth beyond course material. Excluded: casual hobbyists, non-technical producers, AAA studios with dedicated internal tooling teams.", "canonical_hero_copy": "Headline: 'Stop Guessing. Start Building.' — this is the primary value proposition headline for the homepage hero only. It is direct, provocative in the site's established register, and positions the site as a corrective force against guesswork, consistent with the emotional tone directive. Subheadline: 'Rigorous tools, models, and theory for game designers who build real systems and need them to work.' — this names the audience (designers building real systems), signals the methodology (rigorous tools and models), and excludes casual or beginner users implicitly. 'Design with Authority.' is retired as a headline variant and may be repurposed only as supporting copy or a section tagline if needed. Interior pages (contact, tools, guides) must not use the hero component — they should use page-specific, lower-hierarchy headers appropriate to their context.", "core_value_proposition": "Stop Guessing. Start Building. — The canonical headline and positioning statement for GameDesign.uk. Used consistently across all hero components, landing pages, and primary CTAs sitewide. Reflects the site's core promise: replacing guesswork and intuition-based design with rigorous, mathematically grounded tools and theory. Secondary tagline 'Design with Authority.' is retired and should not appear in new content.", "hero_headline_canonical": "Model It Before You Build It. — This headline synthesises the empirical rigour implied by 'Design with Authority' and the action-orientation of 'Start Building. Stop Guessing.' It positions the site as a modelling and simulation layer that precedes production, which is accurate to the toolset (calculators, probability distributions, progression curve models). It carries the punchy declarative fragment style consistent with the established voice, avoids credential-claiming, and creates productive tension by implying the reader's current workflow skips the modelling step. All hero components across index, tool pages, and landing pages should reference this canonical value. The two competing variants should be retired."}	content-gap-planner	content-gap-planner	\N	\N	f	2026-04-07 06:18:11.750384+00	2026-04-07 11:22:44.362765+00	content-gap-planner	f
b3765e9e-09ae-404c-8301-fc9bcd572f27	5fe15466-4e2e-4ff2-981e-98c1b7074002	content_standards	{"hero_content_requirements": "Every page hero must open with at least one verifiable, page-specific proof point before any abstract brand claim. Acceptable proof points include: years in operation (e.g. 'Since 2008'), volume capacity or minimums (e.g. 'Fixed-price contracts from 10,000 litres'), named geographic coverage (e.g. 'Serving forecourts across England and Wales'), named industry accreditations or memberships (e.g. UKIFDA, BFPA, FPS), or a quantified customer or supply metric (e.g. 'Supplying over 400 forecourts'). Generic claims such as 'reliable', 'transparent', or 'competitive' may only appear after a concrete proof point, never as the lead statement. Each page hero should surface a differentiator specific to that page's topic — a fleet services hero should cite a fleet-relevant metric, a pricing page hero should cite a pricing-specific fact — rather than repeating the same company-level tagline across all pages. This standard applies to both headline and subheadline copy in any hero-type component."}	content-gap-planner	content-gap-planner	\N	\N	t	2026-04-07 12:53:18.8293+00	\N	content-gap-planner	f
