neering leads, VP Engineering, and technically sophisticated startup founders evaluating production AI infrastructure. Secondary: forward-thinking business owners who understand technology investment. Tone: Direct, confident, and technically credible — written by builders for builders. No fluff, no vague consulting language. Purpose: Lead generation and consultation bookings for AI agent orchestration engagements, supported by demonstration of deep technical competence through case studies, tooling, and blog content.", "industry_audience_tone_purpose": "Industry: AI infrastructure and enterprise automation. Target Audience: CTOs, engineering leads, and operations directors at mid-market to enterprise companies, plus scaling startup founders evaluating production AI agent deployments. Tone: confident, direct, and technically credible — written by builders for builders, not by consultants for executives. Purpose: generate qualified inbound leads for custom multi-agent system design and production deployment engagements.", "target_audience_tone_and_conversion_goals": "Primary audience: CTOs, VP Engineering, and senior engineering leads at Series B+ startups and mid-to-large enterprises who are actively evaluating or deploying AI agent systems in production. Secondary audience: startup founders and technically-literate business leaders seeking measurable AI automation outcomes. Tone: technical, direct, and peer-to-peer — written by engineers who build real systems, not consultants who theorize. Avoid marketing fluff; lead with architecture decisions, production tradeoffs, and concrete outcomes. Primary conversion goal: discovery call or contact form submission. Secondary goal: blog content engagement and return visits that build trust before outreach.", "industry,target_audience,tone,messaging_pillars": "industry: AI consulting / agent orchestration / technology services. target_audience_primary: CTOs, VP Engineering, and engineering leads at mid-to-large enterprises and scaling startups who are evaluating or actively deploying AI agent systems in production — they are technical, skeptical of hype, and need production-grade answers not theoretical frameworks. target_audience_secondary: Startup founders and forward-thinking business leaders seeking practical AI-driven automation with measurable business outcomes. tone: Direct, technically credible, practitioner-first — written by engineers who ship real systems, not consultants who produce slide decks. No hype, no vague promises. Concrete stack references (Kubernetes, Kafka, Postgres), real failure modes, and honest trade-offs. messaging_pillars: (1) Production reality over proof-of-concept theater — we build systems that survive contact with real workloads. (2) Orchestration as a first-class engineering discipline, not an afterthought. (3) Stack-specific expertise — opinionated choices grounded in operational experience. (4) Measurable outcomes — agent systems justified by business impact, not novelty."}	content-gap-planner	content-gap-planner	\N	\N	f	2026-04-07 03:38:44.161991+00	2026-04-07 13:51:23.297223+00	content-gap-planner	f
8e3c9673-4f4e-444a-8581-5abfef609ae5	15a6cb16-5a86-4541-a8e4-d7106239b6a4	brand_positioning	{"canonical_hero_headline": "Primary headline: 'Design with Authority.' — Subheadline: 'Rigorous tools and theory for game designers who build real systems, balance real economies, and refuse to guess.' — Apply consistently across all hero components on all entry-point pages including index, tools index, guides index, and contact. Retire 'Start Building. Stop Guessing.' entirely. Do not use first person. Do not append exclamation marks. The full stop after 'Authority' is intentional and non-negotiable."}	content-gap-planner	content-gap-planner	\N	\N	t	2026-04-08 11:29:15.761661+00	\N	content-gap-planner	f
e67fef32-3eaa-4bac-88d7-1d04e0523bcf	e1e22a7d-0552-405a-85b3-1b1e51384df5	design_intent	{"avoid": ["Light or white backgrounds — the arena is dark", "Rounded corners that feel soft or friendly — use sharp or subtly rounded for a harder edge", "Gradient backgrounds that feel like generic SaaS or wellness apps", "Hero images with people smiling at cameras", "Decorative serif typography or anything that reads as editorial/magazine", "Grid layouts that look like a social media feed — cards should feel like game tiles, not posts", "Animations that feel like loading states — motion should feel like game feedback, not buffering", "Blue/white corporate colour schemes", "Any design element that could appear on a fintech, HR software, or productivity app"], "colour_mood": "Deep near-black background (#0D0D0F or similar) as the arena floor — creates the feeling of a space that exists outside daylight hours, where something is always live. Primary accent is electric: a charged yellow-green (#C8FF00) or plasma orange (#FF5C00) that reads as energy, not decoration. Secondary accent for Arena/Stage differentiation — a cool electric blue (#00C2FF) for Stage, a hot red-orange for Arena. Colour is used sparingly and purposefully — it marks what matters, not what's pretty. The palette should feel like a scoreboard, not a brand guide.", "style_direction": "bold-creative", "typography_mood": "Primary display font: geometric or grotesque with high x-height and tight tracking — something that reads like a timer or scoreboard at large sizes. Think Druk, Monument Extended, or a condensed grotesque. Body and UI copy: clean mono or neutral sans — functional, fast, readable at small sizes. The type system has two modes: LOUD (headlines, provocations, scores) and quiet (instructions, labels, metadata). No serif anywhere. No decorative fonts. Type should feel like it belongs on a screen in a dark room.", "imagery_direction": "No stock photography. No faces. No lifestyle imagery. Visual language is abstract, kinetic, and data-driven — think heat maps, signal interference, score animations, timer countdowns, reaction arcs. If illustration is used, it should feel like game UI art — clean, geometric, purposeful. Archetype cards are the primary visual artefact — they should look like trading cards or player cards, not social media graphics. Motion and micro-animation over static imagery wherever possible.", "layout_preference": "Mobile-first, card-based, full-bleed. The homepage is a single provocation card filling the viewport — no hero image, no navbar clutter, just the game. Grid layouts for provocation feeds and archetype displays should feel like a game lobby — cards with energy indicators, timers, status badges. Heavy use of negative space on dark backgrounds to make accent elements hit harder. Information hierarchy is game-native: what round, what's at stake, what action to take. No sidebar layouts. No traditional blog-style columns."}	domain-research-classifier	domain-research-classifier	\N	\N	f	2026-04-02 10:57:05.657993+00	2026-04-02 11:46:08.097358+00	domain-research-classifier	f
f03ede2c-4b67-47c0-92ce-7e6a14486da3	e1e22a7d-0552-405a-85b3-1b1e51384df5	content_direction	{"voice": {"person": "Second person singular (you/your) throughout — the platform addresses the player directly and personally. Never 'users' or 'people'. Always 'you'. First person plural (we) avoided — Spark speaks as the game, not a company.", "register": "Charged and direct — the voice of a game announcer who respects the player's intelligence. Every line has weight. Nothing is filler. Reads like a challenge, not a pitch.", "formality": "Deliberately informal but never sloppy. Contractions always. Fragments welcome when they hit harder than full sentences. No corporate vocabulary. No startup clichés. Reads like a smart friend who plays to win.", "emotional_tone": "Competitive, electric, slightly taunting in a playful way. Creates the feeling of walking into an arena where something is already happening. Urgency without anxiety. Confidence without arrogance.", "authority_level": "Establishes authority through the rules of the game, not credentials. Confidence comes from the system design — the game knows what it's doing. Never explains itself defensively. States the mechanic, trusts the player to get it."}, "emphasis": ["The game mechanic — you play, you don't scroll", "Opinions matter here — your take is the entry point", "Identity earned through behaviour, not self-reported", "AI is the game master, humans are the talent", "Daily return habit — the Gauntlet resets every day", "Shareability — your archetype card is your calling card", "This is different from every other platform — rooms not feeds, ephemeral not permanent"], "cta_style": {"approach": "CTAs are game entry points, not conversion asks. They should feel like the velvet rope dropping, not a form submission. Primary CTAs put the player in motion immediately. Secondary CTAs lead to the Gauntlet as the next best action. Never use passive or corporate CTA language.", "verb_choices": "Play, Enter, Take, Drop, Compete, Claim, Show, Step in, Go — active, physical, game-native verbs. Avoid: Sign up, Learn more, Get started, Discover, Explore, Join us."}, "terminology": {"approach": "Game-native vocabulary used consistently and without apology. Terms are introduced through context, not definition — the experience teaches the vocabulary. Avoid social media terminology entirely. Platform-specific terms should feel like they belong to Spark's world, not borrowed from competitors.", "key_terms": ["Provocation — the daily challenge or question that drives play", "Take — a player's submitted opinion or response", "Gauntlet — the daily 5-provocation timed quiz format", "Archetype — the behaviour-emergent identity earned through play", "Arena — competitive mode where takes are judged head-to-head", "Stage — showcase mode for creative and skill-based play", "Room — a live challenge space, not a feed or page", "Game master — the AI's role: framing, scoring, curating", "Round — a single provocation cycle", "Lobby — the pre-play entry state, used for homepage energy"]}, "avoid_phrases": ["Welcome to our website", "We're passionate about", "In today's fast-paced world", "Leveraging AI to", "Our innovative platform", "Join the conversation", "Your voice matters", "Community-driven", "Empowering users", "Seamless experience", "Best-in-class", "Revolutionizing", "Game-changing", "Disruptive", "Web3", "Blockchain", "The future of social", "Sign up for free", "No credit card required", "Lorem ipsum"], "blog_strategy": "Each daily provocation becomes a shareable, indexable content unit at its own URL. These are not blog posts in the traditional sense — they are game cards with a cultural hook, an AI-framed question, and a response prompt. SEO value comes from topical relevance to real-world events. The provocations feed is the blog equivalent, updated daily, with each card optimised for social sharing (Open Graph images, meta descriptions pulled from the provocation text). No long-form editorial content in v1.", "content_depth": {"thoroughness": "Surface-level by design on most pages — the game is the depth. Explanations should be the minimum needed to get someone into the experience. About page goes slightly deeper but still prioritises energy over exhaustiveness.", "assumed_knowledge": "Readers know TikTok, Twitter, Reddit, and Wordle. They understand social platforms and daily games. They don't need the concept of a 'take' explained. They do need to understand what makes Spark different from those things — which is the game mechanic, not the content.", "explanation_pattern": "Contrast first — establish what Spark is NOT before or alongside what it IS. Use platform comparisons as shorthand. Then introduce the mechanic with the minimum words. Let the interface demonstrate the rest."}, "heading_style": {"format": "Imperative or provocative statements preferred over descriptive labels. H1s should read like opening moves in a game — they challenge or claim something. H2s can be slightly more explanatory but still punchy. Avoid heading-as-label pattern (e.g. 'About Our Platform') — use heading-as-statement (e.g. 'The World Is the Content. Your Take Is the Game.').", "hierarchy": "H1 owns the page energy — one per page, maximum impact. H2s structure the experience into named zones that feel like game sections. H3s used only inside content-heavy pages like About or Archetypes. Never use headings as navigation labels — they should add meaning, not just organise."}, "writing_rules": ["Every headline must create tension or pose a challenge — never describe, always provoke", "Never use the word 'content' — say 'takes', 'provocations', 'plays', or 'moves' instead", "Never describe the AI as creative, funny, or opinionated — it frames, scores, and curates only", "The word 'post' is forbidden — users play, respond, submit, enter, or compete", "Avoid 'community' and 'engage' — use 'arena', 'room', 'play', 'compete', 'show up'", "Archetypes are earned, never chosen — copy must never imply users pick or select their archetype", "Timer and ephemeral mechanics must feel like a feature, never a pressure tactic — frame urgency as energy, not scarcity", "Platform comparisons (TikTok/Twitter) are a core rhetorical tool — use them precisely and confidently, never apologetically", "The game metaphor must be consistent across all pages — 'lobby', 'arena', 'stage', 'round', 'play' not 'section', 'area', 'page', 'step'", "CTAs must be action-first and game-specific — 'Enter the Arena', 'Take the Gauntlet', 'Drop Your Take' not 'Sign Up' or 'Learn More'", "Avoid adjective stacking — one strong word beats three weak ones. 'Electric' not 'fun and exciting and engaging'", "When introducing a mechanic, state what it does before what it is — lead with the player experience, not the feature name"], "sentence_style": {"rhythm": "Staccato with occasional longer sentences for contrast. Like a beat drop — short short short, then a line that breathes. Copy should feel like it has tempo.", "average_length": "Short. Punchy. The game moves fast and so does the copy.", "structure_patterns": "Lead with the action or the stakes, not the context. Fragments used for emphasis. Two-sentence rhythm common: setup then hit. Questions used sparingly but effectively — only when they genuinely challenge the reader."}, "example_phrases": {"characteristic": ["The world happened today. What's your take?", "Five provocations. Five minutes. Find out what kind of player you are.", "You don't scroll here. You show up.", "Your archetype isn't chosen. It's earned.", "The AI sets the table. You bring the heat."], "would_never_say": ["Join our growing community of engaged users", "Our AI-powered platform helps you share your thoughts", "Sign up today to start creating content", "We're passionate about connecting people through meaningful conversations", "Discover a new way to engage with trending topics"]}, "paragraph_style": {"structure": "Point first, always. No throat-clearing. No 'In today's world...' openers. State the thing, then support it briefly if needed. Paragraphs should be able to stand alone — each one lands a single idea.", "typical_length": "1-3 sentences maximum for interface-adjacent copy. About and Archetypes pages allow up to 4-5 sentences per paragraph. Never more. If a paragraph needs a sixth sentence, it should be two paragraphs."}, "things_to_avoid": ["Social media vocabulary: 'post', 'scroll', 'feed', 'followers', 'likes', 'content creator', 'influencer'", "Startup clichés: 'disrupting', 'revolutionising', 'changing the game' (ironic given the actual game metaphor), 'next-generation', 'innovative solution'", "AI hype language: never imply the AI is witty, creative, or opinionated — it is infrastructure, not a personality", "Passive or vague CTAs: 'learn more', 'find out how', 'get started', 'explore our platform'", "Corporate warmth: 'we're passionate about', 'our community', 'connecting people', 'bringing people together'", "Overexplaining the mechanics before letting the player experience them — trust the interface", "Dark, violent, or gruesome framing even when provocations are edgy — competitive and adult is not the same as hostile", "Generic social proof: 'millions of users', 'loved by creators', 'trusted by thousands' — unverifiable claims undermine the earned-reputation ethos"], "compliance_rules": [], "things_to_emulate": ["Wordle's zero-explanation entry — the interface teaches the game, copy just opens the door", "The Economist's headline confidence — states a position without hedging, trusts the reader to engage or push back", "Duolingo's streak mechanic language — makes daily return feel like momentum, not obligation", "Sports commentary economy — maximum information, minimum words, always in motion", "The way a great game menu feels — every option is a clear action, nothing is ambiguous, energy is immediate", "Hot Takes Twitter at its best — sharp, specific, committed to the bit without being mean-spirited", "Arcade game copy conventions — score, time, round, level — language that creates stakes without explaining them"], "social_proof_style": "No fabricated testimonials. V1 has no users yet. Social proof comes from the game mechanic itself — show the provocation, show the timer, show the archetype result. The product demonstrates its own value. Future versions will use archetype card shares and Gauntlet completion stats as organic social proof. Never invent user quotes or fake engagement numbers.", "persuasion_approach": {"method": "The game sells itself by being immediately playable. Copy doesn't persuade — it provokes. The CTA is the first provocation card. If the reader has a take, they're already in. Persuasion happens through participation, not description.", "trust_building": "Trust is built through the mechanics being exactly what they say they are. No bait and switch. The timer is real. The archetype is earned. The provocation is genuinely provocative. Credibility comes from the experience being what the copy promised.", "social_proof_style": "Archetype cards shared by real players are the social proof. In v1, lean on the specificity and credibility of the archetypes themselves — if the archetype description rings true, it earns trust. No fake testimonials. No vague 'thousands of players' claims."}}	domain-research-classifier	domain-research-classifier	\N	\N	f	2026-04-02 10:57:03.498717+00	2026-04-02 11:46:10.622762+00	domain-research-classifier	f
30acd4d9-8af5-46e7-b7ff-778591eadad7	e1e22a7d-0552-405a-85b3-1b1e51384df5	identity	{"contact": {"email": null, "phone": null, "address": null, "location": null}, "tagline": "TikTok is where you watch. Twitter is where you shout. Spark is where you play.", "industry": "Digital Entertainment & Social Technology", "services": [{"name": "Daily Gauntlet", "description": "A 5-provocation, 5-minute daily quiz that surfaces your player archetype through your opinions and takes"}, {"name": "Arena Mode", "description": "Competitive take-submission rooms where users go head-to-head on provocations with scoring and outcomes"}, {"name": "Stage Mode", "description": "Creative showcase space for skill-based content with built-in audience and niche discovery"}, {"name": "Provocation Feed", "description": "Daily AI-framed provocations tied to current events, each with a shareable URL and SEO value"}, {"name": "Archetype System", "description": "Behaviour-emergent identity profiles earned through engagement, not self-reported — your reputation builds from how you play"}], "key_people": [], "company_name": "Spark", "sub_industry": "AI-Driven Competitive Opinion Gaming Platform", "about_summary": "Spark is an AI-powered social platform that turns real-world events and trending topics into daily competitive games. Instead of posting or scrolling, users engage by submitting takes on provocations, competing in timed arenas, and earning archetypes based on how they actually play. The AI acts as game master — framing, scoring, and curating — while humans provide all the wit, heat, and creativity.", "social_profiles": {}, "target_audience": "Opinionated, culturally engaged people aged 18-35 who have strong takes on current events but find existing platforms either too passive (TikTok/Instagram) or too chaotic (Twitter/X). Includes creators wanting skill showcase with built-in audience, and curators and tastemakers who discover rather than produce.", "competitors_found": ["twitter.com", "tiktok.com", "reddit.com", "kahoot.com", "nytimes.com/games"], "has_existing_site": false, "existing_site_quality": "none", "unique_selling_points": ["You play, not post — opinion-first entry removes the blank-page barrier", "AI is the game master, never the performer — all wit and heat comes from humans", "Ephemeral challenges build permanent reputation — disposable content, lasting identity", "Archetypes emerge from behaviour, not self-reporting — your profile is earned", "Rooms not feeds — enter a space with energy, don't scroll a stream", "Dual modes: Arena for competitive takes, Stage for creative showcase"]}	domain-research-classifier	domain-research-classifier	\N	\N	f	2026-04-02 10:56:59.516963+00	2026-04-06 20:20:18.360434+00	domain-research-classifier	f
afbb19e1-6f7a-4489-ac6b-7b82d0b19046	e1e22a7d-0552-405a-85b3-1b1e51384df5	classification	{"reasoning": "Spark is not a brochure or portfolio — it IS the product at the landing page level. The index page features a live provocation card with text input and a timer. The Gauntlet is a fully functional client-side JS quiz producing shareable archetype cards. The provocations page serves as both SEO content and live game surface. Every page is either a game interface or a gateway into one. This is an interactive platform in its first static phase, designed to feel like a game lobby from the first pixel.", "site_type": "interactive-platform", "confidence": 0.97, "suggested_style": "arena-dark-vibrant", "tone_suggestion": "game-like", "detected_signals": ["Provocation card with text input on homepage — product IS the landing page", "Client-side gauntlet quiz with archetype result card — interactive JS feature", "Timer mechanic on index — game energy signal", "Shareable archetype cards — viral loop mechanic", "Lobby grid on homepage — arena/game metaphor", "No signup wall on entry — frictionless engagement design", "Dark theme with vibrant accents specified — game UI aesthetic", "Dual Arena/Stage mode architecture — platform depth signal", "AI framing provocations — AI-as-infrastructure, not AI-as-content"], "page_count_estimate": 5, "recommended_builder": "pageflow-builder"}	domain-research-classifier	domain-research-classifier	\N	\N	t	2026-04-02 10:57:01.29724+00	\N	domain-research-classifier	f
059126fc-e649-4a95-a9af-e21cc913da69	e1e22a7d-0552-405a-85b3-1b1e51384df5	content_direction	{"voice": "Direct, confident, slightly provocative. Short sentences. No filler. Speaks to the reader as a player, not a user or customer. Uses game language naturally — play, enter, challenge, round, score — without being cringe or forced. Treats the reader as smart and opinionated. Never explains jokes. Never hedges. The voice has the energy of a great game show host who respects the contestants: clear rules, high stakes, genuine excitement.", "emphasis": ["The game mechanic — you play, you don't scroll", "Opinions matter here — your take is the entry point", "Identity earned through behaviour, not self-reported", "AI is the game master, humans are the talent", "Daily return habit — the Gauntlet resets every day", "Shareability — your archetype card is your calling card", "This is different from every other platform — rooms not feeds, ephemeral not permanent"], "cta_style": {"approach": "CTAs should be action-oriented and specific. Avoid generic labels. Each CTA should reflect the next logical step for the visitor at that point on the page.", "verb_choices": "Use verbs like: Get started, Send a message, Learn more, See how it works, Request a call, Explore — matched to the action and context"}, "terminology": {"approach": "Since the brand's industry is undetermined, use plain English throughout. Once the industry is confirmed, introduce domain-specific terms naturally and define them on first use if there is any chance a visitor won't know them.", "key_terms": ["brand identity", "value proposition", "target audience", "core offering", "contact", "services", "about", "get started", "solution", "partnership"]}, "avoid_phrases": ["Welcome to our website", "We're passionate about", "In today's fast-paced world", "Leveraging AI to", "Our innovative platform", "Join the conversation", "Your voice matters", "Community-driven", "Empowering users", "Seamless experience", "Best-in-class", "Revolutionizing", "Game-changing", "Disruptive", "Web3", "Blockchain", "The future of social", "Sign up for free", "No credit card required", "Lorem ipsum"], "blog_strategy": "Each daily provocation becomes a shareable, indexable content unit at its own URL. These are not blog posts in the traditional sense — they are game cards with a cultural hook, an AI-framed question, and a response prompt. SEO value comes from topical relevance to real-world events. The provocations feed is the blog equivalent, updated daily, with each card optimised for social sharing (Open Graph images, meta descriptions pulled from the provocation text). No long-form editorial content in v1.", "content_depth": {"thoroughness": "Surface-to-mid depth on the main site. Enough to answer 'what is this, who is it for, and why should I care' without overwhelming. Detail reserved for dedicated service or product pages.", "assumed_knowledge": "Assume a general adult audience with no prior knowledge of the brand. Do not assume familiarity with industry-specific terminology unless the brand's niche is established.", "explanation_pattern": "Introduce the concept plainly, then add one layer of supporting detail. Avoid long preambles. Get to the point in the first sentence of any section."}, "heading_style": {"format": "Short, declarative, and benefit-oriented where possible. Headings should complete the thought — not tease it. Avoid question-format headings unless they directly reflect a visitor's real concern.", "hierarchy": "H1 for the single page-level statement. H2 for major sections. H3 for supporting detail within sections. Do not skip levels. Keep H1 to one per page."}, "writing_rules": ["Open every section with the most important idea — do not bury the lead in setup or context", "Use plain English equivalents for any technical or industry-specific term unless the audience is confirmed specialist", "Never use filler phrases such as 'we are pleased to offer', 'cutting-edge', 'world-class', or 'innovative solutions'", "Every CTA should state exactly what happens next — 'Get in touch' is weaker than 'Send us a message and we'll respond within one business day'", "Avoid passive voice — 'We build X' not 'X is built by us'", "Write headlines as outcomes or clear statements, not clever wordplay — clarity wins over wit at this stage", "Keep paragraphs to 2–3 sentences maximum on landing and homepage sections; expand only on dedicated detail pages", "Do not make claims about being 'the best', 'the leading', or 'number one' without verifiable evidence to support them", "Use numerals for all quantities (3 not three) to improve scannability in short-form web copy", "If the brand's niche becomes clear, mirror the language the target audience uses to describe their own problems — not the language the brand uses internally"], "sentence_style": {"rhythm": "Alternates between short declarative statements and slightly longer explanatory sentences. Creates a measured, readable pace that doesn't rush or drag.", "average_length": "Short to medium — typically 10–18 words per sentence. Longer sentences used only to connect two closely related ideas.", "structure_patterns": "Lead with the subject and action. Avoid passive constructions. Use active verbs. One idea per sentence wherever possible."}, "example_phrases": {"characteristic": ["Here's what we do and why it matters to you.", "We built VONC to solve a specific problem — here's what that looks like in practice.", "Get in touch and we'll take it from there.", "Simple to start. Built to last."], "would_never_say": ["We are a passionate team of innovators disrupting the industry.", "Our world-class solutions deliver cutting-edge results for forward-thinking businesses.", "Act now — limited availability!", "We leverage synergies to empower your journey."]}, "paragraph_style": {"structure": "State the point, support it with one detail or example, then either close or transition. Avoid circular paragraphs that restate the opening in different words.", "typical_length": "2–3 sentences for homepage and brochure sections. Up to 4–5 sentences on detail or about pages. Never more than 5 sentences in a single paragraph on a web page."}, "things_to_avoid": ["Buzzwords and empty superlatives: 'innovative', 'disruptive', 'best-in-class', 'synergy'", "Vague mission statements that could apply to any company in any industry", "Stock photography clichés: handshakes, generic skylines, smiling people in offices with no context", "Urgency or scarcity language ('Act now', 'Limited spots', 'Don't miss out') without genuine reason", "Long blocks of text on homepage or landing sections — web visitors scan before they read", "Overpromising outcomes or results that the brand cannot consistently deliver", "Unexplained acronyms or internal jargon before the brand's audience is confirmed", "Navigation structures that bury the most important action behind multiple clicks"], "compliance_rules": [], "things_to_emulate": ["Lead with the user's context or need before introducing the brand's solution", "Use specificity to signal credibility — a concrete detail is more convincing than a broad claim", "Make the primary CTA visible and repeated at logical decision points throughout the page", "Use whitespace generously — a clean, uncluttered layout signals confidence and professionalism", "Write as if explaining to a smart person who has never heard of the brand — no assumed familiarity", "Ensure every page has one clear purpose and one primary action — avoid competing goals on a single page", "Use consistent terminology throughout — do not switch between synonyms for the same concept", "Match the visual hierarchy to the content hierarchy — the most important message should be the most visually prominent"], "social_proof_style": "No fabricated testimonials. V1 has no users yet. Social proof comes from the game mechanic itself — show the provocation, show the timer, show the archetype result. The product demonstrates its own value. Future versions will use archetype card shares and Gauntlet completion stats as organic social proof. Never invent user quotes or fake engagement numbers.", "persuasion_approach": {"method": "Persuade through clarity and specificity. Show what the brand does and why it matters without resorting to pressure tactics. Let the product or service logic do the convincing.", "trust_building": "Trust is built through consistent messaging, clear language, and avoiding overstatement. Specifics outperform generalities — concrete details signal credibility.", "social_proof_style": "If testimonials or case studies exist, present them plainly with real context. Avoid anonymous or vague endorsements. Attribute quotes to real people with relevant roles or outcomes."}}	build-site-planner	build-site-planner	\N	\N	f	2026-04-02 09:58:36.11739+00	2026-04-02 10:57:03.498717+00	build-site-planner	f
4a529d8a-c298-417b-bb2f-1da298345a91	e1e22a7d-0552-405a-85b3-1b1e51384df5	design_intent	{"avoid": ["Corporate blue colour schemes", "Light/white backgrounds", "Stock photography of any kind", "Rounded friendly aesthetics", "Generic social media iconography", "Gradient blobs or mesh gradients", "Thin light-weight typography", "Busy cluttered layouts", "Generic tech startup visual language", "Anything that looks like a SaaS dashboard", "Cartoon or illustration styles", "Rainbow or pride-flag colour palettes used decoratively"], "colour_mood": "Primary background: near-black (#0A0A0F) with charcoal surface layers (#14141F, #1E1E2E). Primary accent: electric violet (#8B5CF6) for interactive elements, CTAs, and energy indicators. Secondary accent: cyan (#06B6D4) for information and secondary actions. Tertiary: hot amber (#F59E0B) for timers, urgency, and scoring. Text: crisp white (#F8FAFC) for headings, cool grey (#94A3B8) for body. The palette should feel like entering a premium digital arena — dark, focused, with bursts of electric colour that draw the eye to action points.", "style_direction": "Dark arena — professional-dark base pushed toward game-energy aesthetics. Not corporate dark, not gamer-bro neon. Think premium esports broadcast meets editorial magazine. Sophisticated but charged.", "typography_mood": "Headings: tight, condensed, geometric sans-serif with strong presence — think Inter Tight, DM Sans, or Geist. All-caps for short labels and CTAs. Body: clean, readable sans-serif at comfortable size. Mono or tabular figures for timers and scores. Overall personality is precise, confident, slightly technical — like a game HUD that's been designed by a luxury brand.", "imagery_direction": "Abstract, atmospheric, arena-themed. Glowing geometric shapes, energy lines, particle effects. Card-like elements floating in dark space. No stock photos of people. No generic tech imagery. No AI-generated faces. Visual language should feel like concept art for a premium game interface. Each archetype should have a distinct abstract symbol or geometric mark, not an illustration of a person.", "layout_preference": "Card-based, mobile-first, generous dark space between elements. Full-bleed hero sections. Cards have subtle borders or glow effects rather than shadows. Grid layouts for collections (provocations, archetypes). Single-column focus for interactive elements (provocation input, gauntlet). Sticky elements for timers and progress. No sidebar layouts. Maximum content width constrained but hero sections go edge-to-edge."}	build-site-planner	build-site-planner	\N	\N	f	2026-04-02 09:58:31.618562+00	2026-04-02 10:57:05.657993+00	build-site-planner	f
7591c0fd-cb15-4f97-a0e0-a445c8710c52	2a8ebf9c-20a2-4c39-b191-840b012371da	tools	{"reasoning": "This site targets CTOs, VP Engineering, and engineering leads at enterprises and scaling startups evaluating production AI agent systems. They are technically sophisticated, skeptical of hype, and need tools that help them make architecture decisions, estimate costs, and self-qualify. Two strong fits exist in the library: the Agent Architecture Complexity Estimator directly serves the core audience by helping them understand what stack they need, and the AI Readiness Quiz serves the secondary audience (business leaders/founders) as a soft qualifier. The ROI Estimator is already deployed. Most other library tools are completely irrelevant to this domain.", "suggestions": [{"name": "Agent Architecture Complexity Estimator", "function": "tool-agent-complexity-estimator", "priority": 1, "complexity": "simple", "description": "Engineering leads describe their target workflow — number of task types, required integrations, concurrency needs, stateful memory requirements — and receive a recommended architecture tier (single agent, supervisor-worker, full hierarchical mesh) with rationale, indicative stack components (Kafka, Postgres, Kubernetes), and a build complexity rating. This directly demonstrates the company's technical depth and helps visitors self-qualify before booking a discovery call.", "target_page": "new", "related_pages": ["services", "building-a-hierarchical-agent-system-with-kafka-and-postgres", "why-most-ai-agent-frameworks-fail-at-the-orchestration-layer"], "library_source": "tool-agent-complexity-estimator", "tool_component_id": "e491f469-52ef-4849-bf96-4dd8dae5002a"}, {"name": "AI Readiness Quiz", "function": "tool-ai-readiness-quiz", "priority": 2, "complexity": "simple", "description": "A short 5–7 question quiz covering business size, current pain points, existing tech stack, and appetite for change. Outputs a plain-English readiness score and personalised summary of where AI agents are most likely to deliver value — or an honest note that they may not be ready yet. Serves the secondary audience of founders and business leaders, builds trust with skeptical visitors, and acts as a soft lead qualifier before they reach the contact page.", "target_page": "new", "related_pages": ["services", "the-enterprise-ai-agent-adoption-gap-2025", "contact"], "library_source": "tool-ai-readiness-quiz", "tool_component_id": "a39145ce-8d95-4dc6-a151-7282bb0a23a5"}, {"name": "LLM Provider Cost Comparison Calculator", "function": "tool-llm-cost-calculator", "priority": 3, "complexity": "moderate", "description": "A custom tool where engineering leads input their expected token volumes (input/output), call frequency, and select from major LLM providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, open-source self-hosted). The tool outputs a monthly and annual cost comparison across providers, highlights break-even points for self-hosting, and surfaces the cost case for provider abstraction. Directly complements the LLM provider abstraction blog post and the multi-agent systems service, giving technical visitors a concrete number to justify architectural decisions.", "target_page": "new", "related_pages": ["llm-provider-abstraction-production-agent-systems", "multi-agent-state-management-distributed-systems", "pricing"], "library_source": null, "tool_component_id": null}], "rejected_tools": [{"reason": "A/B test significance is a marketing/analytics tool. This audience is evaluating agent infrastructure, not conversion rate optimisation.", "function": "tool-ab-test-calculator"}, {"reason": "Midjourney-style prompt builder is for creative image generation. Completely irrelevant to production AI agent orchestration engineering.", "function": "tool-prompt-architect"}, {"reason": "Built for a gamified debate or opinion platform. Has no relevance to enterprise AI infrastructure.", "function": "tool-archetype-clash-calculator"}, {"reason": "Designed for a specific gamified product experience. Not applicable to a B2B AI consulting audience.", "function": "tool-archetype-taster-quiz"}, {"reason": "Demonstrates rating system mathematics. Not relevant to CTOs evaluating agent orchestration infrastructure.", "function": "tool-bayesian-ranking"}, {"reason": "Property finance tool. Entirely outside this industry.", "function": "tool-bridging-loan-calculator"}, {"reason": "Buy-to-let property investment calculator. No relevance to AI agent systems.", "function": "tool-btl-investor"}, {"reason": "Property finance tool. No relevance to this audience.", "function": "tool-equity-release"}, {"reason": "Fleet fuel purchasing tool. Completely outside this domain.", "function": "tool-fuel-cost-estimator"}, {"reason": "Energy measurement conversion. Not relevant to AI consulting.", "function": "tool-gas-unit-converter"}, {"reason": "Industrial robotics end-effector tool. Different engineering domain entirely.", "function": "tool-gripper-payload-calculator"}, {"reason": "Game design tool for loot drop tables. No relevance to enterprise AI orchestration.", "function": "tool-loot-probability-calculator"}, {"reason": "Image editing utility. Not relevant to a technical B2B AI infrastructure audience.", "function": "tool-bg-remover"}, {"reason": "Inappropriate for a professional enterprise-facing AI consultancy.", "function": "tool-meme-generator"}, {"reason": "UK mortgage tool. Entirely outside this industry.", "function": "tool-mortgage-affordability"}, {"reason": "UK mortgage tool. Entirely outside this industry.", "function": "tool-mortgage-overpayment"}, {"reason": "UK mortgage tool. Entirely outside this industry.", "function": "tool-mortgage-repayment"}, {"reason": "Web design utility. While tangentially web-related, this audience is CTOs and engineering leads evaluating infrastructure — not designers needing favicons.", "function": "tool-favicon-generator"}, {"reason": "UK property tax calculator. No relevance to AI agent orchestration."