14a5c748-4a9c-47e4-8d7e-0b7097ee8f71	2a8ebf9c-20a2-4c39-b191-840b012371da	tools	{"reasoning": "This site targets CTOs, VP Engineering, and engineering leads evaluating production AI agent deployments. They are technically sophisticated, skeptical of hype, and need concrete tools that help them make architecture and business decisions. Two tools from the library are directly relevant: the Agent Architecture Complexity Estimator (perfectly aligned with the core service offering) and the AI Readiness Quiz (helps qualify leads and builds trust with skeptical visitors). The ROI Estimator is already deployed. All other library tools are irrelevant to this audience — finance calculators, image tools, gaming tools, and marketing calculators have no place here. One custom tool worth building is an LLM Provider Cost Comparison calculator, since LLM provider abstraction is a recurring theme in their blog and a real decision point for engineering teams evaluating multi-agent systems.", "suggestions": [{"name": "Agent Architecture Complexity Estimator", "function": "tool-agent-complexity-estimator", "priority": 1, "complexity": "simple", "description": "Engineering leads describe their target workflow — number of distinct task types, required integrations, expected concurrency, and whether stateful memory is needed. The tool outputs a recommended architecture tier (single agent, supervisor-worker, full hierarchical mesh) with plain-English rationale, indicative stack components (Kafka, Postgres, Kubernetes), and a ballpark build complexity rating. This directly demonstrates the company's stack depth and helps technical visitors self-qualify before booking a discovery call.", "target_page": "tools", "related_pages": ["services", "why-most-ai-agent-frameworks-fail-at-the-orchestration-layer", "building-a-hierarchical-agent-system-with-kafka-and-postgres"], "library_source": "tool-agent-complexity-estimator", "tool_component_id": "e491f469-52ef-4849-bf96-4dd8dae5002a"}, {"name": "AI Readiness Quiz", "function": "tool-ai-readiness-quiz", "priority": 2, "complexity": "simple", "description": "A short 5–7 question quiz covering business size, current pain points, existing stack, and appetite for change. Outputs a plain-English readiness score and personalised summary of where AI agents are most likely to deliver value — or an honest note if they are not ready yet. This builds trust with skeptical CTOs and engineering leads, acts as a soft lead qualifier, and filters visitors before they reach the contact page. The honesty angle aligns well with the company's 'no demos, no slide decks' positioning.", "target_page": "tools", "related_pages": ["the-enterprise-ai-agent-adoption-gap-2025", "contact", "services"], "library_source": "tool-ai-readiness-quiz", "tool_component_id": "a39145ce-8d95-4dc6-a151-7282bb0a23a5"}, {"name": "LLM Provider Cost Comparison Calculator", "function": "tool-llm-cost-calculator", "priority": 3, "complexity": "moderate", "description": "Lets engineering leads input their expected token volumes (input and output tokens per day), select two or more LLM providers to compare (GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, Gemini 1.5 Pro, etc.), and see projected monthly and annual API costs side by side. Includes a switching-cost sensitivity toggle to show how cost profiles shift if a provider raises prices — reinforcing the case for provider abstraction. Directly supports the existing blog post on LLM provider abstraction and gives technical visitors a concrete, practical reason to engage with the site.", "target_page": "tools", "related_pages": ["llm-provider-abstraction-production-agent-systems", "multi-agent-state-management-distributed-systems", "services"], "library_source": null, "tool_component_id": null}], "rejected_tools": [{"reason": "Targets marketers and ecommerce analysts. The audience here is engineering leadership evaluating infrastructure, not conversion rate optimisation.", "function": "tool-ab-test-calculator"}, {"reason": "A Midjourney-style prompt builder is irrelevant to CTOs and engineering leads evaluating production-grade multi-agent infrastructure.", "function": "tool-prompt-architect"}, {"reason": "Built for a gamified debate or opinion platform. Completely irrelevant to AI consulting and enterprise engineering audiences.", "function": "tool-archetype-clash-calculator"}, {"reason": "Designed for a specific gamified product experience. No relevance to this site or its audience.", "function": "tool-archetype-taster-quiz"}, {"reason": "Interesting statistically but has no connection to AI agent orchestration or the decisions this audience is making.", "function": "tool-bayesian-ranking"}, {"reason": "Financial product calculator for property finance. Entirely off-domain.", "function": "tool-bridging-loan-calculator"}, {"reason": "Buy-to-let property investment tool. No relevance to AI consulting or engineering audiences.", "function": "tool-btl-investor"}, {"reason": "Property finance tool. Completely irrelevant to this site.", "function": "tool-equity-release"}, {"reason": "Fleet and fuel management tool. No connection to AI agent orchestration.", "function": "tool-fuel-cost-estimator"}, {"reason": "Energy sector utility tool. Irrelevant to this audience and industry.", "function": "tool-gas-unit-converter"}, {"reason": "Industrial robotics engineering tool. Not relevant to software-based AI agent orchestration.", "function": "tool-gripper-payload-calculator"}, {"reason": "Game design tool for loot drop systems. Not relevant to enterprise AI consulting.", "function": "tool-loot-probability-calculator"}, {"reason": "Image editing utility. No relevance to the technical B2B audience here.", "function": "tool-bg-remover"}, {"reason": "Inappropriate for a serious enterprise-facing AI consulting firm targeting skeptical CTOs.", "function": "tool-meme-generator"}, {"reason": "Mortgage product calculator. Entirely off-domain.", "function": "tool-mortgage-affordability"}, {"reason": "Mortgage product calculator. Entirely off-domain.", "function": "tool-mortgage-overpayment"}, {"reason": "Mortgage product calculator. Entirely off-domain.", "function": "tool-mortgage-repayment"}, {"reason": "Web design utility. While tangentially web-adjacent, this is not useful for CTOs evaluating AI agent infrastructure.", "function": "tool-favicon-generator"}, {"reason": "UK property tax calculator. Entirely off-domain.", "function": "tool-stamp-duty-calculator"}]}	tool-suggester	tool-suggester	\N	LLM tool evaluation — reasoning, recommendations, and rejections	t	2026-04-08 18:34:22.600095+00	\N	tool-suggester	f
66fc8b77-c51f-4ba1-b35b-66fe8b28b016	15a6cb16-5a86-4541-a8e4-d7106239b6a4	identity	{"tone": "Direct, authoritative, technical, and confident — written for practitioners rather than beginners", "tagline": "Design with Authority.", "industry": "Game Development / Game Design Education", "services": [{"name": "Tools & Calculators", "description": "Browser-based game design calculators for progression curves, combat balance, loot probability, and physics math"}, {"name": "Guides & Theory", "description": "Deep-dives into game design theory, systems thinking, and academic principles covering probability, psychology, economy design, and networking"}, {"name": "Interactive Games", "description": "Interactive prototypes that bring game design concepts to life, including AI pathfinding, network topology, game feel, and economy simulation"}], "adopted_at": "2026-04-03T08:51:48Z", "adopted_from": "gamedesign.uk", "company_name": "GameDesign.uk", "site_identity": "Tagline: 'Rigorous tools and systems theory for game designers who build real things.' | Primary audience: Independent and mid-size studio game designers and systems designers past the beginner stage — building real games, managing real economies, solving real balance problems; comfortable with mathematics, frustrated by guesswork. | Secondary audience: Technically-minded game design students seeking practitioner-level depth beyond course material. | Excluded: casual hobbyists, non-technical producers, AAA studios with dedicated internal tooling. | Primary conversion goal: Tool engagement (calculator usage and return visits) as the top-of-funnel action, with guide readership as the supporting trust-building mechanism and contact form submission as the downstream conversion for consulting or partnership inquiries. | Tone anchor: Senior practitioner speaking to peers — technically specific, direct, challenging without condescension, formal-technical with deliberate punchy fragments; no credentials-claiming, no slang, no exclamation marks.", "site_metadata": "tagline: 'Precision tools for game designers' | industry: 'Game Development / Game Design Education' | target_audience: 'Independent and mid-size studio game designers and systems designers past the beginner stage — building real games, managing real economies, solving real balance problems; secondarily, technically-minded game design students seeking practitioner-level depth' | tone: 'Authoritative and technically precise with deliberate punchy informality — senior practitioner voice, peer-to-peer register, no credentials-claiming, no slang' | purpose: 'Tool usage, applied theory education, and lead generation — positioning GameDesign.uk as the definitive utility resource for rigorous game systems design'", "target_audience": "Primary: Independent and mid-size studio game designers and systems designers who are past the beginner stage — building real games, managing real economies, and solving real balance problems. Comfortable with mathematics, frustrated by guesswork, seeking rigorous and directly applicable tools and theory. Secondary: Technically-minded game design students who are self-directed and seeking practitioner-level depth beyond course material. Excluded: casual hobbyists, non-technical producers, AAA studios with dedicated internal tooling teams.", "canonical_hero_copy": "Headline: 'Stop Guessing. Start Building.' — this is the primary value proposition headline for the homepage hero only. It is direct, provocative in the site's established register, and positions the site as a corrective force against guesswork, consistent with the emotional tone directive. Subheadline: 'Rigorous tools, models, and theory for game designers who build real systems and need them to work.' — this names the audience (designers building real systems), signals the methodology (rigorous tools and models), and excludes casual or beginner users implicitly. 'Design with Authority.' is retired as a headline variant and may be repurposed only as supporting copy or a section tagline if needed. Interior pages (contact, tools, guides) must not use the hero component — they should use page-specific, lower-hierarchy headers appropriate to their context.", "positioning_statement": "GameDesign.uk is a technical resource for independent and mid-size studio game designers who are past the beginner stage and need rigorous, mathematically grounded tools and theory to solve real balance, economy, and systems design problems. It exists to replace guesswork with method — offering calculators, models, and practitioner-level guides that AAA studios build internally and everyone else lacks. It is not a news site, a tutorial aggregator, or a beginner onboarding platform. Its nearest alternatives are GameDeveloper.com (broad, editorial, non-technical) and personal practitioner blogs (deep but fragmented and undiscoverable) — GameDesign.uk occupies the gap between them: structured, tool-first, and technically uncompromising.", "core_value_proposition": "Stop Guessing. Start Building. — The canonical headline and positioning statement for GameDesign.uk. Used consistently across all hero components, landing pages, and primary CTAs sitewide. Reflects the site's core promise: replacing guesswork and intuition-based design with rigorous, mathematically grounded tools and theory. Secondary tagline 'Design with Authority.' is retired and should not appear in new content.", "hero_headline_canonical": "Model It Before You Build It. — This headline synthesises the empirical rigour implied by 'Design with Authority' and the action-orientation of 'Start Building. Stop Guessing.' It positions the site as a modelling and simulation layer that precedes production, which is accurate to the toolset (calculators, probability distributions, progression curve models). It carries the punchy declarative fragment style consistent with the established voice, avoids credential-claiming, and creates productive tension by implying the reader's current workflow skips the modelling step. All hero components across index, tool pages, and landing pages should reference this canonical value. The two competing variants should be retired."}	content-gap-planner	content-gap-planner	\N	\N	f	2026-04-08 11:32:32.010362+00	2026-04-08 19:34:55.874743+00	content-gap-planner	f
